s\":518796958},\"formatted_output\":\"{\\n  \\\"successful\\\": false,\\n  \\\"data\\\": {},\\n  \\\"error\\\": \\\"MCP tool error: {\\\\\\\"ok\\\\\\\":false,\\\\\\\"error\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"code\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"CONTEXT_NOT_FOUND\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"message\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"The context was not found\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"retryable\\\\\\\":false,\\\\\\\"requestId\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"mcp:931b09f4-accb-4b63-ab0a-1046f4391fcf\\\\\\\"}}\\\",\\n  \\\"logId\\\": \\\"log_OwK2pJtxWm-i\\\"\\n}\\n\"},\"started_at_ms\":1789736527145,\"completed_at_ms\":1789736540664}}\n/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/18/rollout-2026-09-18T15-30-27-01a0b3f5-d570-7a72-b668-401efd539e3b.jsonl:2804:{\"timestamp\":\"2026-09-18T13:02:28.174Z\",\"ordinal\":2803,\"type\":\"response_item\",\"payload\":{\"type\":\"custom_tool_call\",\"id\":\"ctc_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aad3661272c87d0875d65d1d11b51b2\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"call_id\":\"call_amWdKTrHsvQd98fFgI7OQHur\",\"name\":\"exec\",\"input\":\"const r = await tools.exec_command({\\n  cmd: \\\"composio execute CUSTOM_SKILLPLANE_SKILL_RETRIEVE -d '{\\\\\\\"skill\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"workspaceSlug\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"21n\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"skillSlug\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"pr-refetch-fix\\\\\\\"},\\\\\\\"version\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"selector\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"revision\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"revision\\\\\\\":4},\\\\\\\"caller\\\\\\\":{\\\\\\\"agentId\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"codex-root\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"agentName\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Codex\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"modelProvider\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"OpenAI\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"modelName\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"GPT-5\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"modelVersion\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"5\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"clientName\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"Codex\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"clientVersion\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"desktop\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"runId\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"pr170-round-20260918\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"sessionId\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"pr170-convergence\\\\\\\",\\\\\\\"conversationId\\\\\\\":\\\\\\\"pr170-convergence\\\\\\\"}}'\\\",\\n  workdir: \\\"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\\\",\\n  yield_time_ms: 30000,\\n  max_output_tokens: 50000\\n});\\ntext(r.output);\\n\",\"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough\":{\"turn_id\":\"01a0b49a-9b09-7382-b6e6-8d1ac944a007\",\"create_time\":1789736541.394889}}}\n/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/18/rollout-2026-09-18T15-30-27-01a0b3f5-d570-7a72-b668-401efd539e3b.jsonl:2806:{\"timestamp\":\"2026-09-18T13:02:36.709Z\",\"ordinal\":2805,\"type\":\"event_msg\",\"payload\":{\"type\":\"item_completed\",\"thread_id\":\"01a0b3f5-d570-7a72-b668-401efd539e3b\",\"turn_id\":\"01a0b49a-9b09-7382-b6e6-8d1ac944a007\",\"item\":{\"type\":\"CommandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-cb06053d-123d-4dbf-9e8b-e293d7a01f4c\",\"process_id\":\"34884\",\"command\":[\"/bin/zsh\",\"-lc\",\"composio execute CUSTOM_SKILLPLANE_SKILL_RETRIEVE -d '{\\\"skill\\\":{\\\"workspaceSlug\\\":\\\"21n\\\",\\\"skillSlug\\\":\\\"pr-refetch-fix\\\"},\\\"version\\\":{\\\"selector\\\":\\\"revision\\\",\\\"revision\\\":4},\\\"caller\\\":{\\\"agentId\\\":\\\"codex-root\\\",\\\"agentName\\\":\\\"Codex\\\",\\\"modelProvider\\\":\\\"OpenAI\\\",\\\"modelName\\\":\\\"GPT-5\\\",\\\"modelVersion\\\":\\\"5\\\",\\\"clientName\\\":\\\"Codex\\\",\\\"clientVersion\\\":\\\"desktop\\\",\\\"runId\\\":\\\"pr170-round-20260918\\\",\\\"sessionId\\\":\\\"pr170-convergence\\\",\\\"conversationId\\\":\\\"pr170-convergence\\\"}}'\"],\"cwd\":\"file:///Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\",\"parsed_cmd\":[{\"type\":\"unknown\",\"cmd\":\"composio execute CUSTOM_SKILLPLANE_SKILL_RETRIEVE -d '{\\\"skill\\\":{\\\"workspaceSlug\\\":\\\"21n\\\",\\\"skillSlug\\\":\\\"pr-refetch-fix\\\"},\\\"version\\\":{\\\"selector\\\":\\\"revision\\\",\\\"revision\\\":4},\\\"caller\\\":{\\\"agentId\\\":\\\"codex-root\\\",\\\"agentName\\\":\\\"Codex\\\",\\\"modelProvider\\\":\\\"OpenAI\\\",\\\"modelName\\\":\\\"GPT-5\\\",\\\"modelVersion\\\":\\\"5\\\",\\\"clientName\\\":\\\"Codex\\\",\\\"clientVersion\\\":\\\"desktop\\\",\\\"runId\\\":\\\"pr170-round-20260918\\\",\\\"sessionId\\\":\\\"pr170-convergence\\\",\\\"conversationId\\\":\\\"pr170-convergence\\\"}}'\"}],\"source\":\"unified_exec_startup\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"stdout\":\"{\\n  \\\"successful\\\": true,\\n  \\\"data\\\": {\\n    \\\"context\\\": null,\\n    \\\"files\\\": [\\n      {\\n        \\\"byteSize\\\": 17699,\\n        \\\"mediaType\\\": \\\"text/markdown; charset=utf-8\\\",\\n        \\\"path\\\": \\\"SKILL.md\\\",\\n        \\\"sha256\\\": \\\"735dafee62d04e1be09123929a0a325972ca4312e1072fd55f4c9ca35e3002c8\\\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \\\"byteSize\\\": 655,\\n        \\\"mediaType\\\": \\\"application/json\\\",\\n        \\\"path\\\": \\\"skill.json\\\",\\n        \\\"sha256\\\": \\\"615c75037c206c8414bf58724fa02c777e91c9e9d8b10f8642a4f7509f5156a8\\\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \\\"instructions\\\": \\\"---\\\\nname: pr-refetch-fix\\\\ndescription: \\\\\\\"Run exactly one bounded GitHub PR review-remediation pass: fetch all pending review surfaces, validate findings against the exact PR worktree, fix current valid issues, verify, push one reviewed fix commit to the PR, report, and stop without any post-push refetch, CI wait, or reviewer loop.\\\\\\\"\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n# PR Refetch Fix\\\\n\\\\nRefetch the current GitHub pull-request review queue, fix the findings that remain valid against the exact PR worktree, verify the affected code, push the fixes to the PR once, report, and stop.\\\\n\\\\n## Absolute single-round boundary\\\\n\\\\nThis skill performs exactly one bounded round per invocation. The bound limits feedback collection and publication, not the completeness of analysis. One round does not mean one comment, one example, or one file. Related defects belong to one complete repair of their shared contract.\\\\n\\\\nOne round is:\\\\n\\\\n1. identify the PR, authentication context, exact head branch, and dedicated worktree\\\\n2. fetch the current review surfaces once\\\\n3. classify the fetched findings against the current worktree\\\\n4. trace each valid root cause across affected paths and fix the complete scoped behavior\\\\n5. run proportionate local verification\\\\n6. when fixes were made, create one reviewed commit and push it to the PR head branch\\\\n7. report the result and stop\\\\n\\\\nAfter step 7, the invocation is complete.\\\\n\\\\nDo not:\\\\n\\\\n- refetch review feedback after the push\\\\n- wait for reviewers or bots to analyze the new commit\\\\n- poll CI until completion\\\\n- begin another fetch/fix/test/push cycle\\\\n- keep working because new feedback arrives asynchronously\\\\n- interpret \\\\\\\"latest\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"complete\\\\\\\", or \\\\\\\"fix pending findings\\\\\\\" as permission for an open-ended loop\\\\n\\\\nA second round requires a new, explicit user invocation or previously granted recurring authorization. Recurring authorization does not waive the convergence safeguards below. If CI or reviewer checks are still running, report them as pending and stop. If no actionable findings are present, report that and stop. If the round is blocked, report the blocker and stop rather than monitoring for a state change.\\\\n\\\\n## Convergence across repeated runs\\\\n\\\\nBefore selecting work, read the prior remediation ledger for this PR. Record the hosted skill identity, version and digest actually used. For recurring workflows, pin the reviewed hosted version in the workflow configuration when authorized; compare local copies with that version and report drift. Do not silently substitute a weaker local copy. A deliberate version upgrade must be recorded. Amending this skill alone does not update running automations.\\\\n\\\\nKeep a compact, persistent task handoff for recurring work; reuse an existing artifact rather than creating one per round. For a single invocation, task context is sufficient. Track snapshot/head SHAs, stable defect-family identifiers, deduplicated valid defects, affected contracts, first-seen and last-attempted rounds, attempted repairs, introduced regressions, dispositions and behavioral evidence. Preserve deferred findings across runs. If history is unavailable, mark the trend unknown and inspect available review/commit history; never infer convergence from missing data.\\\\n\\\\nUse these default reset triggers unless the user explicitly chooses different thresholds:\\\\n\\\\n- The same defect family reappears after two remediation rounds, including newly exposed sibling variants.\\\\n- The number of remaining valid blocking root causes fails to decrease across three consecutive assessed rounds.\\\\n- The user requests convergence, a broader assessment, or a stop to symptom patching: perform the broader assessment immediately rather than waiting for a numeric trigger.\\\\n\\\\nCount root causes, not comments. Do not reset counters by renaming a family, resolving duplicate threads, accepting risk, or narrowing the reported scope. These thresholds trigger reassessment; they are never merge or risk-acceptance thresholds.\\\\n\\\\nWhen triggered, stop routine incremental patch selection. Reassess the affected subsystem against the linked issue and relevant architecture, define the shared invariant, map producers/consumers and failure boundaries, and select a coordinated repair. Continue authorized analysis and implementation; this is not an automatic permission request or an excuse to stop working. If the assessment cannot establish a complete repair, report the unresolved design question or external dependency and do not publish another symptom patch as a completed fix.\\\\n\\\\nFor recurring automation, suspend routine mutation runs when the reset triggers and switch to this assessment. If authorized scheduler controls are available, persist that mode or pause the mutation schedule; otherwise record the reset-required state in the handoff and notify the user. Do not claim the scheduler is paused without confirmation. Resume routine remediation only after recording the revised contract, coordinated plan and verification criteria. Never automatically resume an automation explicitly paused by the user.\\\\n\\\\nDo not continually displace older blockers with the newest bot comment. Related pending findings must be handled together or explicitly leave that contract incomplete. Deferrals require a concrete reason and a next action; convenience, cadence and a desired small commit are not sufficient reasons to split a necessary repair.\\\\n\\\\n## Core review rule\\\\n\\\\nUse current unresolved review threads as the primary queue. Timestamp filtering is secondary.\\\\n\\\\nThe workflow must prevent these failure modes:\\\\n\\\\n- querying only inline comments after a guessed cutoff\\\\n- missing findings in review threads, review bodies, or issue comments\\\\n- ignoring an older unresolved thread that is still valid on the current head\\\\n- treating every unresolved bot comment as valid without inspecting current code\\\\n- fixing against the wrong branch or worktree\\\\n- starting an unbounded post-push review loop\\\\n\\\\n## 1. Identify the PR and authentication context\\\\n\\\\nPreserve the user's established GitHub authentication context for every GitHub command, for example:\\\\n\\\\n```bash\\\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nResolve the repository and PR number from the current branch, worktree, supplied PR URL, or explicit user input.\\\\n\\\\n## 2. Use only the PR's corresponding worktree\\\\n\\\\nBefore fetching comments or editing code:\\\\n\\\\n1. locate the PR's dedicated worktree\\\\n2. confirm its checked-out branch equals the PR head branch\\\\n3. inspect its existing tracked, staged, unstaged, and untracked state\\\\n4. preserve unrelated work and artifacts\\\\n\\\\nMinimum checks:\\\\n\\\\n```bash\\\\ngit branch --show-current\\\\ngit status --short\\\\ngit remote -v\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nTreat that exact worktree as the source of truth for code, validation, staging, and commit decisions. Do not validate comments against a main checkout, base branch, sibling branch, or another PR's worktree.\\\\n\\\\nIf multiple PRs are requested, each PR is a separate round unless the user explicitly defines one bounded multi-PR round.\\\\n\\\\n## 3. Fetch the current review surfaces once\\\\n\\\\nStart with paginated GraphQL review threads. Fetch every page when more than 100 threads may exist.\\\\n\\\\n```bash\\\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api graphql --paginate -f query='\\\\nquery($endCursor: String) {\\\\n  repository(owner: \\\\\\\"OWNER\\\\\\\", name: \\\\\\\"REPO\\\\\\\") {\\\\n    pullRequest(number: PR_NUMBER) {\\\\n      reviewThreads(first: 100, after: $endCursor) {\\\\n        nodes {\\\\n          id\\\\n          isResolved\\\\n          path\\\\n          comments(first: 20) {\\\\n            nodes {\\\\n              body\\\\n              author { login }\\\\n              createdAt\\\\n              outdated\\\\n              url\\\\n              diffHunk\\\\n              originalLine\\\\n              line\\\\n            }\\\\n            pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\\\\n          }\\\\n        }\\\\n        pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\\\\n      }\\\\n    }\\\\n  }\\\\n}'\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nCheck every returned `pageInfo`, including nested comment connections. Do not assume `--paginate` traversed a nested GraphQL connection: explicitly follow its cursor if necessary. Complete every required connection. Record the head SHA and ensure it stays unchanged throughout collection; if it changes, discard the inconsistent snapshot and re-establish the exact head before editing.\\\\n\\\\nThen inspect the supplementary surfaces once:\\\\n\\\\n1. PR review bodies\\\\n2. PR issue comments\\\\n3. raw inline review comments when needed for metadata or export\\\\n\\\\n```bash\\\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh pr view PR_NUMBER --repo OWNER/REPO --json headRefOid,reviews,comments,updatedAt,url\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n```bash\\\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api --paginate repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/PR_NUMBER/comments\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\nDo not replace unresolved-thread discovery with only `gh pr view --json comments`, only `pulls/\u003cn\u003e/comments`, or only a timestamp query.\\\\n\\\\n## 4. Use timestamps only as a secondary lens\\\\n\\\\nIf the user explicitly asks for findings since a push:\\\\n\\\\n1. resolve the exact pushed commit SHA\\\\n2. read its absolute UTC timestamp\\\\n3. use that timestamp only to describe which feedback arrived later\\\\n\\\\nStill include an older unresolved thread when it remains actionable on the current head.\\\\n\\\\n## 5. Classify root causes before editing\\\\n\\\\nKeep a compact working ledger keyed by root cause: thread IDs/URLs, snapshot SHA, affected paths, current-code evidence, status, and verification. Keep it in task context for a single pass; persist it in the reusable task handoff for recurring runs as described above.\\\\n\\\\nClassify every relevant finding as exactly one of:\\\\n\\\\n- `Actionable`: a current defect within scope that can be addressed in this round.\\\\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale)`: current-code evidence shows the behavior is fixed, superseded, or no longer applicable. An outdated hunk alone is not proof; trace its current implementation.\\\\n- `Invalid`: current-code or behavioral evidence disproves the reported defect. Record the evidence, not just disagreement with the reviewer.\\\\n- `Accepted Risk`: a verified defect explicitly accepted by the user or an identified authorized decision-maker. Record the decision, scope, impact and follow-up. The agent cannot use this label to clear unfinished work; accepted risks remain visible and do not count as fixes.\\\\n- `Not Attempted`: a blocked current defect, out-of-scope concern, or external/non-code gate. Distinguish these reasons. A valid blocked defect remains unresolved; do not count it as clean.\\\\n\\\\nDeduplicate comments reporting the same root cause. An unchanged old comment is not evidence that its issue is fixed. Reuse prior verified evidence only when the relevant code and contract remain unchanged.\\\\n\\\\n## 6. Establish the affected contract\\\\n\\\\nFor each actionable root cause, before editing:\\\\n\\\\n1. State the behavior that must hold and the concrete failing case.\\\\n2. Trace its producers, consumers, callers, and sibling implementations in the PR worktree. Inspect equivalent entrypoints such as CLI versus batch operations, gateway versus worker execution, and normal versus retry/recovery paths when relevant.\\\\n3. Identify where the invariant is enforced and what the proposed change could break. For stateful changes, consider ownership, fencing, ordering, partial failure, and cleanup where they affect this contract.\\\\n4. Record which paths need changes, which already satisfy the contract, and the behavioral evidence needed to verify the fix.\\\\n5. Make assumptions and exclusions explicit. For security or serialization, identify the protected data, threat model, final consumer-visible boundary and permitted structural output. Resolve contradictory or impossible guarantees before implementation. A substring match, for example, needs analysis of data provenance and the promised artifact contract before being classified as disclosure.\\\\n\\\\nThe unit of remediation is the root cause and its affected contract, not the commented line. A directly related defect discovered during this inspection belongs to the same scoped batch even when no reviewer named that line. Record its connection to the snapshot finding. Unrelated discoveries remain out of scope.\\\\n\\\\n## 7. Implement the smallest complete repair\\\\n\\\\nRepair every applicable path identified above. Prefer a shared enforcement point when it simplifies correctness; do not introduce an abstraction solely to satisfy this procedure.\\\\n\\\\nPreserve unrelated dirty work and exclude unrelated untracked and `.conduct/` artifacts. Do not expand into repository-wide cleanup or redesign. If a complete repair requires expanded authority or an unavailable dependency, explain the remaining defect and classify it as blocked rather than presenting a symptom patch as complete.\\\\n\\\\n## 8. Verify behavior and review the fix batch\\\\n\\\\nFor each root cause, demonstrate the previously failing behavior and its corrected outcome with focused regression coverage where practical. Confirm the test would fail on the old behavior, by running it against the old code when safe or by explaining the distinguishing assertion. Do not claim a reproduced failure when it was only reasoned through.\\\\n\\\\nUse evidence appropriate to the contract. Verify consumer-visible outcomes at the relevant boundaries: serialized artifacts, cleanup recover…20028 tokens truncated…-visible outcomes at the relevant boundaries: serialized artifacts, cleanup recovery, supported package entrypoints, or export/replay. Exercise combinations and composition where failures arise from interacting inputs; use property-based or generated cases when justified, without requiring them for every small fix. A regression matrix must test the stated invariant, not merely enumerate examples from bot comments.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nUse evidence appropriate to the contract:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- For SQL, claims, locks, and transactions, prove resulting state and relevant concurrency behavior using a real disposable database when feasible. SQL-text assertions alone do not establish correctness.\\\\\\\\n- For retry/recovery, cover the relevant interrupted state and the evidence needed to declare success.\\\\\\\\n- For configuration propagation, verify both the executing component's configuration and required secret wiring without deploying.\\\\\\\\n- Tests that mutate fixtures must restore prior values and row existence or use isolated disposable fixtures.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nIf behavioral verification is unavailable, report the gap explicitly. Test counts, mocks that mirror the implementation, and typechecks are not substitutes for proof of the failing behavior.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nAfter editing, review the entire fix diff together with its callers and affected contract before staging. Check for missed sibling paths, newly broken consumers, error/cleanup regressions, and interactions between fixes. Re-run checks when justified by subsequent changes or unresolved concerns. Complete local repair and verification within this single pre-push batch.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nRun focused tests first, then proportionate typecheck, lint, test, and build gates. Distinguish change-caused failures, baseline/environment limits, local proof, and external state.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nDo not wait for external CI in this skill. A single immediate check snapshot is allowed for reporting; no polling. Skipped, failed, or absent reviewers do not prove coverage.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## 9. Commit and push the fixes exactly once\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nInvoking this skill authorizes one reviewed commit and one normal push of the fixes produced by this round to the PR head branch. Do not leave completed fixes only in the local worktree. If the fetched snapshot contains no actionable findings and no changes were made, do not create an empty commit or push.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nBefore committing:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. review the exact staged allowlist\\\\\\\\n2. run `git diff --cached --check`\\\\\\\\n3. confirm unrelated artifacts are excluded\\\\\\\\n4. confirm the root-cause ledger identifies evidence for every claimed fix and lists remaining valid defects\\\\\\\\n5. confirm no reset trigger remains unaddressed and no claimed complete repair leaves a known affected path deferred\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nCreate exactly one commit for the reviewed fix batch and push it once to the PR head branch. Do not force-push. Verify the local, remote, and PR head SHAs once after pushing.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nDo not refetch review feedback after the push. Do not wait for the pushed commit to receive new reviews.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## 10. Reconcile the queue\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nSeparate remaining code defects from open GitHub thread count. Report how many snapshot threads are fixed/already fixed, still valid, blocked, or unassessed, distinguishing thread counts from deduplicated root causes.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThis skill does not by itself authorize posting replies, resolving threads, or dismissing reviews. If separately authorized by the user, resolve only snapshot threads whose full concern is covered by current-code evidence and the verified PR head; use captured IDs and mutation responses without refetching feedback. Never resolve a valid blocked finding or dismiss a review merely to reduce the count. Otherwise report fixed threads as eligible for resolution while leaving their GitHub state unchanged.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## 11. Report and terminate\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nReport with these explicit buckets:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- `Fixed`\\\\\\\\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale)`\\\\\\\\n- `Not Attempted`\\\\\\\\n- `Verification`\\\\\\\\n- `Invalid / Accepted Risk` when applicable\\\\\\\\n- `Convergence`: valid blocking root causes before/after, newly discovered defects, fix-induced regressions, recurring families, oldest deferred blockers and verification gaps. Distinguish assessed from unassessed items. Use improving, stalled, regressing or unknown with evidence; more tests, commits or resolved threads alone do not establish progress.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nFor each fixed root cause, summarize affected paths and behavioral evidence. Include unverified contracts and valid blocked defects; a completed round does not establish merge readiness.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nInclude the exact pushed commit SHA and PR URL. If no changes were needed, say explicitly that no commit or push was created. State any pending CI or reviewer checks without waiting for them.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nEnd with a clear termination statement such as:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n\u003e One review round is complete. No post-push refetch or additional review loop was performed.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThen stop.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\",\\\\n    \\\\\\\"requestId\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"mcp:9447489e-a117-423c-ad6b-faa5c7137ef2\\\\\\\",\\\\n    \\\\\\\"skill\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"description\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Run exactly one bounded GitHub PR review-remediation pass: fetch all pending review surfaces, validate findings against the exact PR worktree, fix only current valid issues, verify, optionally commit and push once when authorized, report, and stop without any post-push refetch, CI wait, or reviewer loop.\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"skill:3ba0f6dd-212c-4933-9feb-cf87481a0466\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"name\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"PR Refetch Fix\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"slug\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"pr-refetch-fix\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"tags\\\\\\\": [\\\\n        \\\\\\\"code-review\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"github\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"pull-request\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"remediation\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"single-pass\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ],\\\\n      \\\\\\\"visibility\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"workspace\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"workspaceId\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"workspace:cf0aa9c5-76c8-4a89-8a9b-66c70676c298\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"workspaceSlug\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"21n\\\\\\\"\\\\n    },\\\\n    \\\\\\\"version\\\\\\\": {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"byteSize\\\\\\\": 7678,\\\\n      \\\\\\\"createdAt\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"2026-09-15T07:41:54.717Z\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"digest\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"sha256:8781c1c12fe9d5902337d6cf21bfbac49dd3a94c7eadd46bf2622db350444022\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"id\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"skill-version:e741e530-df30-485d-9253-affc90d6ac56\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"manifest\\\\\\\": {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"byteSize\\\\\\\": 7678,\\\\n        \\\\\\\"digest\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"sha256:8781c1c12fe9d5902337d6cf21bfbac49dd3a94c7eadd46bf2622db350444022\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"expandedByteSize\\\\\\\": 18354,\\\\n        \\\\\\\"fileCount\\\\\\\": 2,\\\\n        \\\\\\\"files\\\\\\\": [\\\\n          {\\\\n            \\\\\\\"byteSize\\\\\\\": 17699,\\\\n            \\\\\\\"mediaType\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"text/markdown; charset=utf-8\\\\\\\",\\\\n            \\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SKILL.md\\\\\\\",\\\\n            \\\\\\\"sha256\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"735dafee62d04e1be09123929a0a325972ca4312e1072fd55f4c9ca35e3002c8\\\\\\\"\\\\n          },\\\\n          {\\\\n            \\\\\\\"byteSize\\\\\\\": 655,\\\\n            \\\\\\\"mediaType\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"application/json\\\\\\\",\\\\n            \\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"skill.json\\\\\\\",\\\\n            \\\\\\\"sha256\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"615c75037c206c8414bf58724fa02c777e91c9e9d8b10f8642a4f7509f5156a8\\\\\\\"\\\\n          }\\\\n        ],\\\\n        \\\\\\\"formatVersion\\\\\\\": 1\\\\n      },\\\\n      \\\\\\\"publishedAt\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"2026-09-15T07:52:37.513Z\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"revision\\\\\\\": 4,\\\\n      \\\\\\\"semanticVersion\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"1.2.0\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"state\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"published\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  },\\\\n  \\\\\\\"error\\\\\\\": null,\\\\n  \\\\\\\"logId\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"log_zIamgy-LtvJZ\\\\\\\"\\\\n}\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"duration\\\":{\\\"secs\\\":9,\\\"nanos\\\":723461250},\\\"formatted_output\\\":\\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"successful\\\\\\\": true,\\\\n  \\\\\\\"data\\\\\\\": {\\\\n    \\\\\\\"context\\\\\\\": null,\\\\n    \\\\\\\"files\\\\\\\": [\\\\n      {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"byteSize\\\\\\\": 17699,\\\\n        \\\\\\\"mediaType\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"text/markdown; charset=utf-8\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SKILL.md\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"sha256\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"735dafee62d04e1be09123929a0a325972ca4312e1072fd55f4c9ca35e3002c8\\\\\\\"\\\\n      },\\\\n      {\\\\n        \\\\\\\"byteSize\\\\\\\": 655,\\\\n        \\\\\\\"mediaType\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"application/json\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"path\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"skill.json\\\\\\\",\\\\n        \\\\\\\"sha256\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"615c75037c206c8414bf58724fa02c777e91c9e9d8b10f8642a4f7509f5156a8\\\\\\\"\\\\n      }\\\\n    ],\\\\n    \\\\\\\"instructions\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"---\\\\\\\\nname: pr-refetch-fix\\\\\\\\ndescription: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"Run exactly one bounded GitHub PR review-remediation pass: fetch all pending review surfaces, validate findings against the exact PR worktree, fix current valid issues, verify, push one reviewed fix commit to the PR, report, and stop without any post-push refetch, CI wait, or reviewer loop.\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\\n---\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n# PR Refetch Fix\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nRefetch the current GitHub pull-request review queue, fix the findings that remain valid against the exact PR worktree, verify the affected code, push the fixes to the PR once, report, and stop.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Absolute single-round boundary\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThis skill performs exactly one bounded round per invocation. The bound limits feedback collection and publication, not the completeness of analysis. One round does not mean one comment, one example, or one file. Related defects belong to one complete repair of their shared contract.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nOne round is:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. identify the PR, authentication context, exact head branch, and dedicated worktree\\\\\\\\n2. fetch the current review surfaces once\\\\\\\\n3. classify the fetched findings against the current worktree\\\\\\\\n4. trace each valid root cause across affected paths and fix the complete scoped behavior\\\\\\\\n5. run proportionate local verification\\\\\\\\n6. when fixes were made, create one reviewed commit and push it to the PR head branch\\\\\\\\n7. report the result and stop\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nAfter step 7, the invocation is complete.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nDo not:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- refetch review feedback after the push\\\\\\\\n- wait for reviewers or bots to analyze the new commit\\\\\\\\n- poll CI until completion\\\\\\\\n- begin another fetch/fix/test/push cycle\\\\\\\\n- keep working because new feedback arrives asynchronously\\\\\\\\n- interpret \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"latest\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"complete\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", or \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"fix pending findings\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" as permission for an open-ended loop\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nA second round requires a new, explicit user invocation or previously granted recurring authorization. Recurring authorization does not waive the convergence safeguards below. If CI or reviewer checks are still running, report them as pending and stop. If no actionable findings are present, report that and stop. If the round is blocked, report the blocker and stop rather than monitoring for a state change.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Convergence across repeated runs\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nBefore selecting work, read the prior remediation ledger for this PR. Record the hosted skill identity, version and digest actually used. For recurring workflows, pin the reviewed hosted version in the workflow configuration when authorized; compare local copies with that version and report drift. Do not silently substitute a weaker local copy. A deliberate version upgrade must be recorded. Amending this skill alone does not update running automations.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nKeep a compact, persistent task handoff for recurring work; reuse an existing artifact rather than creating one per round. For a single invocation, task context is sufficient. Track snapshot/head SHAs, stable defect-family identifiers, deduplicated valid defects, affected contracts, first-seen and last-attempted rounds, attempted repairs, introduced regressions, dispositions and behavioral evidence. Preserve deferred findings across runs. If history is unavailable, mark the trend unknown and inspect available review/commit history; never infer convergence from missing data.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nUse these default reset triggers unless the user explicitly chooses different thresholds:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- The same defect family reappears after two remediation rounds, including newly exposed sibling variants.\\\\\\\\n- The number of remaining valid blocking root causes fails to decrease across three consecutive assessed rounds.\\\\\\\\n- The user requests convergence, a broader assessment, or a stop to symptom patching: perform the broader assessment immediately rather than waiting for a numeric trigger.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nCount root causes, not comments. Do not reset counters by renaming a family, resolving duplicate threads, accepting risk, or narrowing the reported scope. These thresholds trigger reassessment; they are never merge or risk-acceptance thresholds.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nWhen triggered, stop routine incremental patch selection. Reassess the affected subsystem against the linked issue and relevant architecture, define the shared invariant, map producers/consumers and failure boundaries, and select a coordinated repair. Continue authorized analysis and implementation; this is not an automatic permission request or an excuse to stop working. If the assessment cannot establish a complete repair, report the unresolved design question or external dependency and do not publish another symptom patch as a completed fix.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nFor recurring automation, suspend routine mutation runs when the reset triggers and switch to this assessment. If authorized scheduler controls are available, persist that mode or pause the mutation schedule; otherwise record the reset-required state in the handoff and notify the user. Do not claim the scheduler is paused without confirmation. Resume routine remediation only after recording the revised contract, coordinated plan and verification criteria. Never automatically resume an automation explicitly paused by the user.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nDo not continually displace older blockers with the newest bot comment. Related pending findings must be handled together or explicitly leave that contract incomplete. Deferrals require a concrete reason and a next action; convenience, cadence and a desired small commit are not sufficient reasons to split a necessary repair.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## Core review rule\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nUse current unresolved review threads as the primary queue. Timestamp filtering is secondary.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThe workflow must prevent these failure modes:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- querying only inline comments after a guessed cutoff\\\\\\\\n- missing findings in review threads, review bodies, or issue comments\\\\\\\\n- ignoring an older unresolved thread that is still valid on the current head\\\\\\\\n- treating every unresolved bot comment as valid without inspecting current code\\\\\\\\n- fixing against the wrong branch or worktree\\\\\\\\n- starting an unbounded post-push review loop\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## 1. Identify the PR and authentication context\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nPreserve the user's established GitHub authentication context for every GitHub command, for example:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```bash\\\\\\\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nResolve the repository and PR number from the current branch, worktree, supplied PR URL, or explicit user input.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## 2. Use only the PR's corresponding worktree\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nBefore fetching comments or editing code:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. locate the PR's dedicated worktree\\\\\\\\n2. confirm its checked-out branch equals the PR head branch\\\\\\\\n3. inspect its existing tracked, staged, unstaged, and untracked state\\\\\\\\n4. preserve unrelated work and artifacts\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nMinimum checks:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```bash\\\\\\\\ngit branch --show-current\\\\\\\\ngit status --short\\\\\\\\ngit remote -v\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nTreat that exact worktree as the source of truth for code, validation, staging, and commit decisions. Do not validate comments against a main checkout, base branch, sibling branch, or another PR's worktree.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nIf multiple PRs are requested, each PR is a separate round unless the user explicitly defines one bounded multi-PR round.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## 3. Fetch the current review surfaces once\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStart with paginated GraphQL review threads. Fetch every page when more than 100 threads may exist.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```bash\\\\\\\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api graphql --paginate -f query='\\\\\\\\nquery($endCursor: String) {\\\\\\\\n  repository(owner: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"OWNER\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\", name: \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"REPO\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\") {\\\\\\\\n    pullRequest(number: PR_NUMBER) {\\\\\\\\n      reviewThreads(first: 100, after: $endCursor) {\\\\\\\\n        nodes {\\\\\\\\n          id\\\\\\\\n          isResolved\\\\\\\\n          path\\\\\\\\n          comments(first: 20) {\\\\\\\\n            nodes {\\\\\\\\n              body\\\\\\\\n              author { login }\\\\\\\\n              createdAt\\\\\\\\n              outdated\\\\\\\\n              url\\\\\\\\n              diffHunk\\\\\\\\n              originalLine\\\\\\\\n              line\\\\\\\\n            }\\\\\\\\n            pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\\\\\\\\n          }\\\\\\\\n        }\\\\\\\\n        pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\\\\\\\\n      }\\\\\\\\n    }\\\\\\\\n  }\\\\\\\\n}'\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nCheck every returned `pageInfo`, including nested comment connections. Do not assume `--paginate` traversed a nested GraphQL connection: explicitly follow its cursor if necessary. Complete every required connection. Record the head SHA and ensure it stays unchanged throughout collection; if it changes, discard the inconsistent snapshot and re-establish the exact head before editing.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nThen inspect the supplementary surfaces once:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. PR review bodies\\\\\\\\n2. PR issue comments\\\\\\\\n3. raw inline review comments when needed for metadata or export\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```bash\\\\\\\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh pr view PR_NUMBER --repo OWNER/REPO --json headRefOid,reviews,comments,updatedAt,url\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n```bash\\\\\\\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api --paginate repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/PR_NUMBER/comments\\\\\\\\n```\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nDo not replace unresolved-thread discovery with only `gh pr view --json comments`, only `pulls/\u003cn\u003e/comments`, or only a timestamp query.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## 4. Use timestamps only as a secondary lens\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nIf the user explicitly asks for findings since a push:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n1. resolve the exact pushed commit SHA\\\\\\\\n2. read its absolute UTC timestamp\\\\\\\\n3. use that timestamp only to describe which feedback arrived later\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nStill include an older unresolved thread when it remains actionable on the current head.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n## 5. Classify root causes before editing\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nKeep a compact working ledger keyed by root cause: thread IDs/URLs, snapshot SHA, affected paths, current-code evidence, status, and verification. Keep it in task context for a single pass; persist it in the reusable task handoff for recurring runs as described above.\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\nClassify every relevant finding as exactly one of:\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n- `Actionable`: a current defect within scope that can be addressed in this round.\\\\\\\\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale)`: current-code evidence shows the behavior is fixed, superseded, or no longer applicable. An outdated hunk alone is not proof; trace its current implementation.\\\\\\\\n- `Invalid`: current-code or behavioral evidence disproves the reported defect. Record the evidence, not just disagreement with the reviewer.\\\\\\\\n- `Accepted Risk`: a verified defect explicitly accepted by the user or an identified authorized decision-maker. Record the decision, scope, impact and follow-utail: stdout\\n\"}],\"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough\":{\"turn_id\":\"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433\",\"create_time\":1789742659.606315}},\"metadata\":{\"client_authored\":false,\"fallback_token_limit_override\":12000}}\n/Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/18/rollout-2026-09-18T15-30-27-01a0b3f5-d570-7a72-b668-401efd539e3b.jsonl:3821:{\"timestamp\":\"2026-09-18T14:44:26.612Z\",\"ordinal\":3820,\"type\":\"response_item\",\"payload\":{\"type\":\"custom_tool_call\",\"id\":\"ctc_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aad4e48caf887d093191331eea083d4\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"call_id\":\"call_PIAbaNqdaicqAG299gz2ym7x\",\"name\":\"exec\",\"input\":\"const r = await tools.exec_command({\\n  cmd: \\\"rg -n --glob '*.jsonl' 'CUSTOM_SKILLPLANE_SKILL_RETRIEVE.*selector.*revision' /Users/serro/.codex/sessions/2026/09/18 2\u003e/dev/null | tail -n 10\\\",\\n  workdir: \\\"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\\\",\\n  yield_time_ms: 10000,\\n  max_output_tokens: 30000\\n});\\ntext(r.output);\",\"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough\":{\"turn_id\":\"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433\",\"create_time\":1789742660.820549}}}\n","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aad4e4cb2b087d0b358ad6f6bdf53ea","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"summary":[{"text":"**Executing with correct run IDs**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"call_id":"call_iign37ceGcydrnnY4xHfNrlk","id":"ctc_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aad4e4d883c87d0a77ef4da287ab54c","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({\n  cmd: \"composio execute CUSTOM_SKILLPLANE_SKILL_RETRIEVE -d '{\\\"skill\\\":{\\\"workspaceSlug\\\":\\\"21n\\\",\\\"skillSlug\\\":\\\"pr-refetch-fix\\\"},\\\"version\\\":{\\\"selector\\\":\\\"revision\\\",\\\"revision\\\":4},\\\"caller\\\":{\\\"agentId\\\":\\\"codex-root\\\",\\\"agentName\\\":\\\"Codex\\\",\\\"modelProvider\\\":\\\"OpenAI\\\",\\\"modelName\\\":\\\"GPT-5\\\",\\\"modelVersion\\\":\\\"5\\\",\\\"clientName\\\":\\\"Codex\\\",\\\"clientVersion\\\":\\\"desktop\\\",\\\"runId\\\":\\\"pr170-round9-retry-20260918\\\",\\\"sessionId\\\":\\\"pr170-round9-retry-20260918\\\",\\\"conversationId\\\":\\\"pr170-round9-retry-20260918\\\"}}'\",\n  workdir: \"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\",\n  yield_time_ms: 30000,\n  max_output_tokens: 50000\n});\ntext(r.output);","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789742667.70161,"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_iign37ceGcydrnnY4xHfNrlk","id":"ctco_01a0b4fa-0d6f-7f13-b1a4-079fff58fc96","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789742681.455382,"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 8.6 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"{\n  \"successful\": true,\n  \"data\": {\n    \"context\": null,\n    \"files\": [\n      {\n        \"byteSize\": 17699,\n        \"mediaType\": \"text/markdown; charset=utf-8\",\n        \"path\": \"SKILL.md\",\n        \"sha256\": \"735dafee62d04e1be09123929a0a325972ca4312e1072fd55f4c9ca35e3002c8\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"byteSize\": 655,\n        \"mediaType\": \"application/json\",\n        \"path\": \"skill.json\",\n        \"sha256\": \"615c75037c206c8414bf58724fa02c777e91c9e9d8b10f8642a4f7509f5156a8\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"instructions\": \"---\\nname: pr-refetch-fix\\ndescription: \\\"Run exactly one bounded GitHub PR review-remediation pass: fetch all pending review surfaces, validate findings against the exact PR worktree, fix current valid issues, verify, push one reviewed fix commit to the PR, report, and stop without any post-push refetch, CI wait, or reviewer loop.\\\"\\n---\\n\\n# PR Refetch Fix\\n\\nRefetch the current GitHub pull-request review queue, fix the findings that remain valid against the exact PR worktree, verify the affected code, push the fixes to the PR once, report, and stop.\\n\\n## Absolute single-round boundary\\n\\nThis skill performs exactly one bounded round per invocation. The bound limits feedback collection and publication, not the completeness of analysis. One round does not mean one comment, one example, or one file. Related defects belong to one complete repair of their shared contract.\\n\\nOne round is:\\n\\n1. identify the PR, authentication context, exact head branch, and dedicated worktree\\n2. fetch the current review surfaces once\\n3. classify the fetched findings against the current worktree\\n4. trace each valid root cause across affected paths and fix the complete scoped behavior\\n5. run proportionate local verification\\n6. when fixes were made, create one reviewed commit and push it to the PR head branch\\n7. report the result and stop\\n\\nAfter step 7, the invocation is complete.\\n\\nDo not:\\n\\n- refetch review feedback after the push\\n- wait for reviewers or bots to analyze the new commit\\n- poll CI until completion\\n- begin another fetch/fix/test/push cycle\\n- keep working because new feedback arrives asynchronously\\n- interpret \\\"latest\\\", \\\"complete\\\", or \\\"fix pending findings\\\" as permission for an open-ended loop\\n\\nA second round requires a new, explicit user invocation or previously granted recurring authorization. Recurring authorization does not waive the convergence safeguards below. If CI or reviewer checks are still running, report them as pending and stop. If no actionable findings are present, report that and stop. If the round is blocked, report the blocker and stop rather than monitoring for a state change.\\n\\n## Convergence across repeated runs\\n\\nBefore selecting work, read the prior remediation ledger for this PR. Record the hosted skill identity, version and digest actually used. For recurring workflows, pin the reviewed hosted version in the workflow configuration when authorized; compare local copies with that version and report drift. Do not silently substitute a weaker local copy. A deliberate version upgrade must be recorded. Amending this skill alone does not update running automations.\\n\\nKeep a compact, persistent task handoff for recurring work; reuse an existing artifact rather than creating one per round. For a single invocation, task context is sufficient. Track snapshot/head SHAs, stable defect-family identifiers, deduplicated valid defects, affected contracts, first-seen and last-attempted rounds, attempted repairs, introduced regressions, dispositions and behavioral evidence. Preserve deferred findings across runs. If history is unavailable, mark the trend unknown and inspect available review/commit history; never infer convergence from missing data.\\n\\nUse these default reset triggers unless the user explicitly chooses different thresholds:\\n\\n- The same defect family reappears after two remediation rounds, including newly exposed sibling variants.\\n- The number of remaining valid blocking root causes fails to decrease across three consecutive assessed rounds.\\n- The user requests convergence, a broader assessment, or a stop to symptom patching: perform the broader assessment immediately rather than waiting for a numeric trigger.\\n\\nCount root causes, not comments. Do not reset counters by renaming a family, resolving duplicate threads, accepting risk, or narrowing the reported scope. These thresholds trigger reassessment; they are never merge or risk-acceptance thresholds.\\n\\nWhen triggered, stop routine incremental patch selection. Reassess the affected subsystem against the linked issue and relevant architecture, define the shared invariant, map producers/consumers and failure boundaries, and select a coordinated repair. Continue authorized analysis and implementation; this is not an automatic permission request or an excuse to stop working. If the assessment cannot establish a complete repair, report the unresolved design question or external dependency and do not publish another symptom patch as a completed fix.\\n\\nFor recurring automation, suspend routine mutation runs when the reset triggers and switch to this assessment. If authorized scheduler controls are available, persist that mode or pause the mutation schedule; otherwise record the reset-required state in the handoff and notify the user. Do not claim the scheduler is paused without confirmation. Resume routine remediation only after recording the revised contract, coordinated plan and verification criteria. Never automatically resume an automation explicitly paused by the user.\\n\\nDo not continually displace older blockers with the newest bot comment. Related pending findings must be handled together or explicitly leave that contract incomplete. Deferrals require a concrete reason and a next action; convenience, cadence and a desired small commit are not sufficient reasons to split a necessary repair.\\n\\n## Core review rule\\n\\nUse current unresolved review threads as the primary queue. Timestamp filtering is secondary.\\n\\nThe workflow must prevent these failure modes:\\n\\n- querying only inline comments after a guessed cutoff\\n- missing findings in review threads, review bodies, or issue comments\\n- ignoring an older unresolved thread that is still valid on the current head\\n- treating every unresolved bot comment as valid without inspecting current code\\n- fixing against the wrong branch or worktree\\n- starting an unbounded post-push review loop\\n\\n## 1. Identify the PR and authentication context\\n\\nPreserve the user's established GitHub authentication context for every GitHub command, for example:\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other\\n```\\n\\nResolve the repository and PR number from the current branch, worktree, supplied PR URL, or explicit user input.\\n\\n## 2. Use only the PR's corresponding worktree\\n\\nBefore fetching comments or editing code:\\n\\n1. locate the PR's dedicated worktree\\n2. confirm its checked-out branch equals the PR head branch\\n3. inspect its existing tracked, staged, unstaged, and untracked state\\n4. preserve unrelated work and artifacts\\n\\nMinimum checks:\\n\\n```bash\\ngit branch --show-current\\ngit status --short\\ngit remote -v\\n```\\n\\nTreat that exact worktree as the source of truth for code, validation, staging, and commit decisions. Do not validate comments against a main checkout, base branch, sibling branch, or another PR's worktree.\\n\\nIf multiple PRs are requested, each PR is a separate round unless the user explicitly defines one bounded multi-PR round.\\n\\n## 3. Fetch the current review surfaces once\\n\\nStart with paginated GraphQL review threads. Fetch every page when more than 100 threads may exist.\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api graphql --paginate -f query='\\nquery($endCursor: String) {\\n  repository(owner: \\\"OWNER\\\", name: \\\"REPO\\\") {\\n    pullRequest(number: PR_NUMBER) {\\n      reviewThreads(first: 100, after: $endCursor) {\\n        nodes {\\n          id\\n          isResolved\\n          path\\n          comments(first: 20) {\\n            nodes {\\n              body\\n              author { login }\\n              createdAt\\n              outdated\\n              url\\n              diffHunk\\n              originalLine\\n              line\\n            }\\n            pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\\n          }\\n        }\\n        pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n}'\\n```\\n\\nCheck every returned `pageInfo`, including nested comment connections. Do not assume `--paginate` traversed a nested GraphQL connection: explicitly follow its cursor if necessary. Complete every required connection. Record the head SHA and ensure it stays unchanged throughout collection; if it changes, discard the inconsistent snapshot and re-establish the exact head before editing.\\n\\nThen inspect the supplementary surfaces once:\\n\\n1. PR review bodies\\n2. PR issue comments\\n3. raw inline review comments when needed for metadata or export\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh pr view PR_NUMBER --repo OWNER/REPO --json headRefOid,reviews,comments,updatedAt,url\\n```\\n\\n```bash\\nGH_CONFIG_DIR=~/.config/gh-other gh api --paginate repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls/PR_NUMBER/comments\\n```\\n\\nDo not replace unresolved-thread discovery with only `gh pr view --json comments`, only `pulls/\u003cn\u003e/comments`, or only a timestamp query.\\n\\n## 4. Use timestamps only as a secondary lens\\n\\nIf the user explicitly asks for findings since a push:\\n\\n1. resolve the exact pushed commit SHA\\n2. read its absolute UTC timestamp\\n3. use that timestamp only to describe which feedback arrived later\\n\\nStill include an older unresolved thread when it remains actionable on the current head.\\n\\n## 5. Classify root causes before editing\\n\\nKeep a compact working ledger keyed by root cause: thread IDs/URLs, snapshot SHA, affected paths, current-code evidence, status, and verification. Keep it in task context for a single pass; persist it in the reusable task handoff for recurring runs as described above.\\n\\nClassify every relevant finding as exactly one of:\\n\\n- `Actionable`: a current defect within scope that can be addressed in this round.\\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale)`: current-code evidence shows the behavior is fixed, superseded, or no longer applicable. An outdated hunk alone is not proof; trace its current implementation.\\n- `Invalid`: current-code or behavioral evidence disproves the reported defect. Record the evidence, not just disagreement with the reviewer.\\n- `Accepted Risk`: a verified defect explicitly accepted by the user or an identified authorized decision-maker. Record the decision, scope, impact and follow-up. The agent cannot use this label to clear unfinished work; accepted risks remain visible and do not count as fixes.\\n- `Not Attempted`: a blocked current defect, out-of-scope concern, or external/non-code gate. Distinguish these reasons. A valid blocked defect remains unresolved; do not count it as clean.\\n\\nDeduplicate comments reporting the same root cause. An unchanged old comment is not evidence that its issue is fixed. Reuse prior verified evidence only when the relevant code and contract remain unchanged.\\n\\n## 6. Establish the affected contract\\n\\nFor each actionable root cause, before editing:\\n\\n1. State the behavior that must hold and the concrete failing case.\\n2. Trace its producers, consumers, callers, and sibling implementations in the PR worktree. Inspect equivalent entrypoints such as CLI versus batch operations, gateway versus worker execution, and normal versus retry/recovery paths when relevant.\\n3. Identify where the invariant is enforced and what the proposed change could break. For stateful changes, consider ownership, fencing, ordering, partial failure, and cleanup where they affect this contract.\\n4. Record which paths need changes, which already satisfy the contract, and the behavioral evidence needed to verify the fix.\\n5. Make assumptions and exclusions explicit. For security or serialization, identify the protected data, threat model, final consumer-visible boundary and permitted structural output. Resolve contradictory or impossible guarantees before implementation. A substring match, for example, needs analysis of data provenance and the promised artifact contract before being classified as disclosure.\\n\\nThe unit of remediation is the root cause and its affected contract, not the commented line. A directly related defect discovered during this inspection belongs to the same scoped batch even when no reviewer named that line. Record its connection to the snapshot finding. Unrelated discoveries remain out of scope.\\n\\n## 7. Implement the smallest complete repair\\n\\nRepair every applicable path identified above. Prefer a shared enforcement point when it simplifies correctness; do not introduce an abstraction solely to satisfy this procedure.\\n\\nPreserve unrelated dirty work and exclude unrelated untracked and `.conduct/` artifacts. Do not expand into repository-wide cleanup or redesign. If a complete repair requires expanded authority or an unavailable dependency, explain the remaining defect and classify it as blocked rather than presenting a symptom patch as complete.\\n\\n## 8. Verify behavior and review the fix batch\\n\\nFor each root cause, demonstrate the previously failing behavior and its corrected outcome with focused regression coverage where practical. Confirm the test would fail on the old behavior, by running it against the old code when safe or by explaining the distinguishing assertion. Do not claim a reproduced failure when it was only reasoned through.\\n\\nUse evidence appropriate to the contract. Verify consumer-visible outcomes at the relevant boundaries: serialized artifacts, cleanup recovery, supported package entrypoints, or export/replay. Exercise combinations and composition where failures arise from interacting inputs; use property-based or generated cases when justified, without requiring them for every small fix. A regression matrix must test the stated invariant, not merely enumerate examples from bot comments.\\n\\nUse evidence appropriate to the contract:\\n\\n- For SQL, claims, locks, and transactions, prove resulting state and relevant concurrency behavior using a real disposable database when feasible. SQL-text assertions alone do not establish correctness.\\n- For retry/recovery, cover the relevant interrupted state and the evidence needed to declare success.\\n- For configuration propagation, verify both the executing component's configuration and required secret wiring without deploying.\\n- Tests that mutate fixtures must restore prior values and row existence or use isolated disposable fixtures.\\n\\nIf behavioral verification is unavailable, report the gap explicitly. Test counts, mocks that mirror the implementation, and typechecks are not substitutes for proof of the failing behavior.\\n\\nAfter editing, review the entire fix diff together with its callers and affected contract before staging. Check for missed sibling paths, newly broken consumers, error/cleanup regressions, and interactions between fixes. Re-run checks when justified by subsequent changes or unresolved concerns. Complete local repair and verification within this single pre-push batch.\\n\\nRun focused tests first, then proportionate typecheck, lint, test, and build gates. Distinguish change-caused failures, baseline/environment limits, local proof, and external state.\\n\\nDo not wait for external CI in this skill. A single immediate check snapshot is allowed for reporting; no polling. Skipped, failed, or absent reviewers do not prove coverage.\\n\\n## 9. Commit and push the fixes exactly once\\n\\nInvoking this skill authorizes one reviewed commit and one normal push of the fixes produced by this round to the PR head branch. Do not leave completed fixes only in the local worktree. If the fetched snapshot contains no actionable findings and no changes were made, do not create an empty commit or push.\\n\\nBefore committing:\\n\\n1. review the exact staged allowlist\\n2. run `git diff --cached --check`\\n3. confirm unrelated artifacts are excluded\\n4. confirm the root-cause ledger identifies evidence for every claimed fix and lists remaining valid defects\\n5. confirm no reset trigger remains unaddressed and no claimed complete repair leaves a known affected path deferred\\n\\nCreate exactly one commit for the reviewed fix batch and push it once to the PR head branch. Do not force-push. Verify the local, remote, and PR head SHAs once after pushing.\\n\\nDo not refetch review feedback after the push. Do not wait for the pushed commit to receive new reviews.\\n\\n## 10. Reconcile the queue\\n\\nSeparate remaining code defects from open GitHub thread count. Report how many snapshot threads are fixed/already fixed, still valid, blocked, or unassessed, distinguishing thread counts from deduplicated root causes.\\n\\nThis skill does not by itself authorize posting replies, resolving threads, or dismissing reviews. If separately authorized by the user, resolve only snapshot threads whose full concern is covered by current-code evidence and the verified PR head; use captured IDs and mutation responses without refetching feedback. Never resolve a valid blocked finding or dismiss a review merely to reduce the count. Otherwise report fixed threads as eligible for resolution while leaving their GitHub state unchanged.\\n\\n## 11. Report and terminate\\n\\nReport with these explicit buckets:\\n\\n- `Fixed`\\n- `Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale)`\\n- `Not Attempted`\\n- `Verification`\\n- `Invalid / Accepted Risk` when applicable\\n- `Convergence`: valid blocking root causes before/after, newly discovered defects, fix-induced regressions, recurring families, oldest deferred blockers and verification gaps. Distinguish assessed from unassessed items. Use improving, stalled, regressing or unknown with evidence; more tests, commits or resolved threads alone do not establish progress.\\n\\nFor each fixed root cause, summarize affected paths and behavioral evidence. Include unverified contracts and valid blocked defects; a completed round does not establish merge readiness.\\n\\nInclude the exact pushed commit SHA and PR URL. If no changes were needed, say explicitly that no commit or push was created. State any pending CI or reviewer checks without waiting for them.\\n\\nEnd with a clear termination statement such as:\\n\\n\u003e One review round is complete. No post-push refetch or additional review loop was performed.\\n\\nThen stop.\\n\",\n    \"requestId\": \"mcp:1286d26a-0059-4365-8850-beeb787a946b\",\n    \"skill\": {\n      \"description\": \"Run exactly one bounded GitHub PR review-remediation pass: fetch all pending review surfaces, validate findings against the exact PR worktree, fix only current valid issues, verify, optionally commit and push once when authorized, report, and stop without any post-push refetch, CI wait, or reviewer loop.\",\n      \"id\": \"skill:3ba0f6dd-212c-4933-9feb-cf87481a0466\",\n      \"name\": \"PR Refetch Fix\",\n      \"slug\": \"pr-refetch-fix\",\n      \"tags\": [\n        \"code-review\",\n        \"github\",\n        \"pull-request\",\n        \"remediation\",\n        \"single-pass\"\n      ],\n      \"visibility\": \"workspace\",\n      \"workspaceId\": \"workspace:cf0aa9c5-76c8-4a89-8a9b-66c70676c298\",\n      \"workspaceSlug\": \"21n\"\n    },\n    \"version\": {\n      \"byteSize\": 7678,\n      \"createdAt\": \"2026-09-15T07:41:54.717Z\",\n      \"digest\": \"sha256:8781c1c12fe9d5902337d6cf21bfbac49dd3a94c7eadd46bf2622db350444022\",\n      \"id\": \"skill-version:e741e530-df30-485d-9253-affc90d6ac56\",\n      \"manifest\": {\n        \"byteSize\": 7678,\n        \"digest\": \"sha256:8781c1c12fe9d5902337d6cf21bfbac49dd3a94c7eadd46bf2622db350444022\",\n        \"expandedByteSize\": 18354,\n        \"fileCount\": 2,\n        \"files\": [\n          {\n            \"byteSize\": 17699,\n            \"mediaType\": \"text/markdown; charset=utf-8\",\n            \"path\": \"SKILL.md\",\n            \"sha256\": \"735dafee62d04e1be09123929a0a325972ca4312e1072fd55f4c9ca35e3002c8\"\n          },\n          {\n            \"byteSize\": 655,\n            \"mediaType\": \"application/json\",\n            \"path\": \"skill.json\",\n            \"sha256\": \"615c75037c206c8414bf58724fa02c777e91c9e9d8b10f8642a4f7509f5156a8\"\n          }\n        ],\n        \"formatVersion\": 1\n      },\n      \"publishedAt\": \"2026-09-15T07:52:37.513Z\",\n      \"revision\": 4,\n      \"semanticVersion\": \"1.2.0\",\n      \"state\": \"published\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"error\": null,\n  \"logId\": \"log_dgppea4mJwka\"\n}\n","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aad4e5bee1c87d0b4d3a56dbed3d9a9","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"summary":[{"text":"**Continuing manual workflow run**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Planning full review data retrieval**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Preparing temp storage for review data**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"content":[{"text":"The direct execution retry succeeded. The hosted workflow exactly matches `1.2.0`, revision `4`, and digest `sha256:8781…4022`; I’ve read the full live instructions. I’m resuming the manual bounded round now. The automation remains disabled from the earlier safety stop—I will not silently re-enable it—and I’ll take exactly one complete review/check snapshot at the confirmed PR head.","type":"output_text"}],"id":"msg_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aad4e65c51087d0b1013ff00674ad32","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["unknown"],"create_time":1789742682.178672,"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"phase":"commentary","role":"assistant","type":"message"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aad4e66e10487d083eb92395500a113","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"summary":[{"text":"**Planning full ledger read with line limit**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"call_id":"call_BEIKudB3CF1iTs4zWB32D5as","id":"ctc_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aad4e683a3c87d085020c86a29c0451","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({\n  cmd: \"wc -l .conduct/pr-170-remediation-ledger.md\\nsed -n '1,460p' .conduct/pr-170-remediation-ledger.md\",\n  workdir: \"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\",\n  yield_time_ms: 10000,\n  max_output_tokens: 50000\n});\ntext(r.output);","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789742682.178672,"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_BEIKudB3CF1iTs4zWB32D5as","id":"ctco_01a0b4fa-4e4a-7f42-a524-3afa8cd5ae65","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789742698.058968,"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.4 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 15236)\nTotal output lines: 411\n\n     410 .conduct/pr-170-remediation-ledger.md\n# PR 170 recurring remediation ledger\n\n## Workflow identity\n\n- Hosted skill: `21n/pr-refetch-fix@1.2.0`\n- Revision: `4`\n- Digest: `sha256:8781c1c12fe9d5902337d6cf21bfbac49dd3a94c7eadd46bf2622db350444022`\n- Automation: `4442e7f3-60f4-4125-a8e4-c818ac40fc82`\n- Repository / PR: `21nCo/super-functions#170`\n- Head branch: `codex/mcp-1-transport-neutral-conformance-0910`\n\n## Current round\n\n- Round: `8`\n- Mode: `assessment` (credential-boundary and CLI runtime-output families recurred; coordinated reassessment completed before mutation)\n- Local pre-snapshot HEAD: `0e89647cb6a20681e546a22c1d2c58eabe74253e`\n- Snapshot / PR head: `0e89647cb6a20681e546a22c1d2c58eabe74253e`\n- Snapshot consistency: stable across all seven review-thread pages, five review pages, four raw-inline pages, issue comments, PR metadata, and the immediate check snapshot; all observed heads matched\n- Review surfaces: 321 threads, 217 review records, 64 issue comments, and 360 raw inline comments; every connection/page was terminal and all nested thread-comment connections were terminal\n- Commit / push budget: used exactly once each\n- Round commit: `dfaeef0d1a4b25cc2ae9e10ccde0481b255637ad` (`fix(mcpfn): close report output gaps`)\n- Push: one normal push to `origin/codex/mcp-1-transport-neutral-conformance-0910`\n- One allowed post-push head comparison: local = remote branch = PR head = `dfaeef0d1a4b25cc2ae9e10ccde0481b255637ad`; PR remained open\n\n## Broader subsystem assessment\n\n- Shared invariant: credential-bearing MCP operations must preserve retryable resource ownership until cleanup succeeds, never expose opaque credential material at any consumer-visible boundary, and keep authored report/event structure intact. Conformance evidence must reject insecure OAuth token envelopes, regression tests must assert the behavior they claim, and CI bootstrap must not execute source-distribution setup code.\n- Producers and consumers: credential providers and authenticated targets produce leases and target-owned redaction; `McpFnClient` owns transports/events/diagnostics; testing suites and official conformance produce bounded reports; inspector produces snapshots/scenarios; `runCli` persists operator artifacts; `bin.ts` is the only process-termination boundary. Hosted OAuth servers produce redirect/token responses consumed by the authorization regression runner. GitHub Actions consumes Python bootstrap packages.\n- Failure boundaries: acquisition/open/initialize, inventory and scenario execution, payload/envelope projection, report serialization, protocol/transport/revoke/dispose cleanup, programmatic CLI error propagation, executable stderr flush/termination, authorization-code exchange and refresh, and package bootstrap in CI.\n- Coordinated repair: (1) persist any bounded cleanup-failure artifact, then propagate the owning cleanup error through `runCli`; retry once at the outer library boundary; only `bin.ts` prints a safe diagnostic and terminates for all three retryable cleanup error types; (2) force payload mode for every inspector snapshot projection; (3) validate JSON plus `Cache-Control: no-store` and `Pragma: no-cache` for both exchange and refresh success responses; (4) correct misleading/incorrect regressions; (5) require binary Python distributions in the failing Sonar-gated bootstrap step. These are complete enforcement points for the current findings rather than commented-line patches.\n- Verification criteria: focused old-behavior regressions for cleanup retry failure/success, executable diagnostics, snapshot payload collisions, key-only scenario redaction, exchange/refresh cache headers, and misleading retry configuration; then package typechecks/tests, the Node 22 McpFn release gate, `git diff --check`, and one pre-push review of the complete diff. Hosted checks are observed only in the already captured immediate snapshot.\n\n### Round 2 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: consumer-visible reports and snapshots must never silently remain passing when credential safety or evidence completeness cannot be guaranteed, and executable termination must not discard already-produced evidence.\n- Producers / consumers: authenticated target credential leases and the shared scrubber produce protected values and redacted artifacts; target suites, inspector and JUnit consume authored structural envelopes; official conformance produces captured streams; `serializeBoundedReport` persists bounded operator evidence; `runCli` writes reports; `bin.ts` owns forced process termination.\n- Failure boundaries: credential text colliding with authored discriminator values, final JSON recomposition, report-size truncation, asynchronous stdout backpressure, cleanup-owner propagation into the executable boundary, and non-enumerable `Error.message` / `cause` fields in regressions.\n- Coordinated repair: reject structural-value credential collisions at the shared scrubber while preserving authored schema keys; mark truncated conformance reports explicitly incomplete and force exit 1; await every CLI output write before cleanup retry/termination; assert cleanup error messages and reports separately.\n- Verification criteria: collision regressions for `pass` / `complete` and existing structural values; a successful oversized conformance result must persist as bounded `status: incomplete`, `ok: false`, exit 1; delayed stdout must complete before cleanup retry can reject; the cleanup assertion must inspect both message and report. Then package typechecks/tests, the full Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 3 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: a retryable cleanup failure must retain the exact resource owner until cleanup succeeds; later output, diagnostic, persistence, or sanitization failures may be reported only after ownership is safely preserved. Public-client OAuth fixtures must distinguish protocol-compliant token rejection from an authentication challenge.\n- Producers / consumers: authenticated conformance and target suites produce retryable cleanup-owner errors; `McpFnInspector` owns an `McpFnClient` transport; command actions produce stdout/stderr and persisted reports; `runCli` performs the one bounded retry; `bin.ts` is the final process boundary. Hosted authorization fixtures produce token exchange/refresh rejections consumed by the conformance runner.\n- Failure boundaries: inspector close after operation/output failure, conformance and target-suite output/report persistence after a cleanup artifact exists, outer diagnostic output before cleanup classification/retry, and unauthenticated token exchange/refresh status validation.\n- Coordinated repair: add a retryable inspector cleanup owner; prioritize every cleanup owner over secondary output/persistence/diagnostic failures; preserve that owner through the bounded retry and executable termination boundary; and require HTTP 400 for `invalid_client` from public-client exchange and refresh fixtures.\n- Verification criteria: regressions must show inspector ownership survives two failed closes and can later be retried; conformance and target-suite owners must survive rejecting output callbacks and report persistence failures; executable classification must include inspector cleanup; public-client `invalid_client` must pass at 400 and fail at 401 for exchange and refresh. Then focused tests/typechecks, the Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 4 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: while a target owns credentials, every consumer-visible dynamic value—including reconstructed schema discriminators—must be proven safe by that target's redactor before emission; if mandatory structure collides with a credential, the producer must fail closed or emit a safe marked omission. Any abstraction that exposes `close()` must retain and return the retry owner when close fails.\n- Producers / consumers: `McpFnClient` produces diagnostic and client-event envelopes consumed directly and by `McpFnInspector`; the inspector reconstructs snapshot, timeline, and scenario discriminators for library and CLI consumers. `McpFnTestClient` owns a production `McpFnClient`; direct callers and `mcpfn test` consume its public `close()` contract, while `runCli` already recognizes `McpFnTestClientCleanupError`.\n- Failure boundaries: structural values reconstructed after payload redaction (`kind`, `phase`, `outcome`, `clientState`, target kind, scenario side effect), safe omission fallback construction, inspector snapshot/export assembly, and post-connect local test-client shutdown after successful operations.\n- Coordinated repair: add one client structural-safety enforcement point backed by the active target redactor; apply it to event/diagnostic discriminators and all inspector-reconstructed discriminators, dropping only when no schema-safe omission can be produced; then make `McpFnTestClient.close()` wrap retained session cleanup in the existing retryable owner while preserving connection-failure cause semantics during failed connect cleanup.\n- Verification criteria: credentials equal to client/inspector structural values must never appear in direct events, timelines, snapshots, or scenario exports; safe omissions must be marked and counted, and mandatory snapshot/export collisions must reject with credential-free errors. A post-connect test-client close must retain the same owner through repeated failure and later succeed, and the CLI's existing owner retry boundary must remain green. Then affected package tests/typechecks, the Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 5 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: every emitted event or diagnostic must preserve both credential confidentiality and schema semantics. A timestamp may be emitted only as the exact generated ISO string after the active target redactor proves that string safe; redaction must never turn it into a malformed timestamp.\n- Producers / consumers: `McpFnClient.emitEvent()` and diagnostic dispatch produce `at`; direct event/diagnostic listeners, `McpFnInspector` timelines/snapshots, target-suite timelines, CLI serialization, and mixed package consumers retain it.\n- Failure boundaries: timestamp generation, active-target structural safety validation, construction of a marked omission fallback, and the no-safe-fallback case where every ISO timestamp collides with a credential.\n- Coordinated repair: enforce one ISO timestamp structural validator in the shared client producer; use it for normal client events, normal diagnostics, and both omission fallbacks. On collision, route through existing omission accounting; emit a marked fallback only with a safe valid ISO timestamp, otherwise drop the artifact while retaining the omission count.\n- Verification criteria: an exact timestamp credential must never appear and must produce a marked omission whose `at` round-trips through `Date.toISOString()`; a credential present in every ISO representation must emit no malformed artifact and must increment the appropriate omission counter. Then client tests/typecheck, the complete Node 22 McpFn release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 6 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: every terminal cleanup failure must retain both its retry owner and its actual failure phase. Transport shutdown is `transport-close`; OAuth revocation is `token-revocation`; disposal after successful revocation returns to `transport-close`. A generic outer cleanup boundary must not overwrite a more specific trusted phase.\n- Producers / consumers: the authenticated HTTP target owns the credential lease and produces target diagnostics; `McpFnClient` coordinates handle/target cleanup and produces retryable client errors; `McpFnTestClient` retains the close owner; target-suite diagnostics, bounded reports, JUnit, CLI, and direct diagnostic consumers classify the resulting phase and failure layer.\n- Failure boundaries: raw transport close before credential release, OAuth `revoke()`, provider `dispose()`, client cleanup-drain wrapping, test-client error wrapping, and final suite report normalization.\n- Coordinated repair: throw a phase-bearing safe cleanup error from the credential lease; emit the lease phase only after transport shutdown succeeds; validate and preserve `token-revocation` through the client cleanup coordinator; and normalize the retained inner cleanup error in the target suite so a later generic close diagnostic cannot replace the terminal phase.\n- Verification criteria: direct target diagnostics must report transport failure as `transport-close`, OAuth revoke failure as `token-revocation`, and post-revocation disposal failure as `transport-close`; suite reports must map those phases to `authorization-server` and `mcpfn-preflight` respectively while retaining a retryable cleanup owner. Then client/testing focused tests and typechecks, the complete Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 7 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: credential cleanup errors may expose only a trusted phase, safe fixed text, and retry ownership; raw provider failures must not cross the lease, target, client, suite, inspector, or CLI boundary. Every independently reflectable credential component, including structured Cookie values, must be registered before artifacts are emitted. Target-suite post-execution artifact I/O failures are runtime failures and must not replace a retained cleanup owner.\n- Producers / consumers: credential providers produce header material and revoke/dispose failures; `credentialValues` and target secret scopes produce the redaction set; credential leases and authenticated targets own retryable cleanup; `McpFnClient` coordinates target/handle cleanup; suites, JUnit, inspector, and CLI serialize consumer-visible evidence. The target suite produces reports consumed by `runCli`, whose output writers and executable boundary classify failures.\n- Failure boundaries: structured Cookie parsing, pre-handle header validation/open failure, revoke/dispose rejection, target-level pending-release retries, client cleanup-drain/close wrapping, report/JUnit/stdout persistence, and final exit-code classification.\n- Coordinated repair: extract and validate Cookie credential components at the shared secret-discovery point; sanitize lease cleanup failures and client cleanup wrappers while preserving the trusted phase separately; rethrow the retained safe rejection from target-level cleanup instead of synthesizing `transport-close`; and convert target-suite artifact/output failures into a safe runtime error without displacing any cleanup owner.\n- Verification criteria: a reflected Cookie value must be absent from direct redaction and JSON/JUnit artifacts; a provider error containing a credential must not be reachable through cleanup cause chains; repeated OAuth revoke failure during a pre-handle open failure must remain `token-revocation` through client/suite reporting and later retry; target report/JUnit/stdout write failures without a cleanup owner must return exit 1 with safe text, while owner-priority regressions remain green. Then affected package tests/typechecks, the complete Node 22 McpFn release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 8 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: every target-controlled value that occupies authored report structure must be proven unchanged by the active live-session redactor before emission; a structural collision must make the report safely incomplete. Every post-execution conformance output or persistence failure is an operational failure with fixed safe text and exit 1, while an existing retryable cleanup owner remains authoritative.\n- Producers / consumers: custom targets produce arbitrary `describe().kind` values and target-owned redaction; the live `McpFnClient` owns the active redactor and structural-preservation check; the target suite reconstructs bounded JSON/JUnit artifacts consumed by libraries and `mcpfn test-target`. Official conformance produces captured streams and bounded reports consumed by `runCli`, stdout/stderr callbacks, and report persistence.\n- Failure boundaries: suite projection after scenario execution but before credential cleanup; reinsertion of the raw target discriminator after descriptor payload redaction; final JSON/JUnit serialization; conformance stdout/stderr callbacks and report-file writes; cleanup-owner priority over secondary I/O failures.\n- Coordinated repair: validate the dynamic target kind with the live client's shared `preserveArtifactStructure` enforcement before reconstructing the descriptor so a collision sets the existing projection-failure path; apply the existing cleanup-aware safe runtime-output wrapper to the complete conformance stdout/stderr/report operation with a conformance-specific fixed message.\n- Verification criteria: a custom target whose redactor treats its arbitrary kind as a credential must return a bounded incomplete report with the raw kind absent; conformance report, stdout, and stderr failures without a cleanup owner must return exit 1 with fixed safe text and omit raw I/O details, while the existing cleanup-owner priority regressions remain green. Then affected package tests/typechecks/builds, the complete Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n## Defect-family ledger\n\n| Stable ID | Root cause / affected contract | Disposition | First seen | Last attempted | Attempted repairs | Regression / verification evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `MCP170-CREDENTIAL-BOUNDARY` | Credential-safe redaction across reconstructed targets, diagnostics, and emitted artifacts | Fixed in round 7 batch; recurring family | pre-ledger | round 7 | Prior `1e45b794…` plus round 7 structured-Cookie component extraction/fail-closed parsing | Thread `PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jvkZ0`; direct reflected Cookie values are now removed and ambiguous syntax is rejected; testing suite and release gate pass |\n| `MCP170-CLEANUP-PHASE` | Dynamic revoke/dispose cleanup phase and safe CLI failure handling | Fixed in round 7 batch; recurring family | pre-ledger | round 7 | Prior `1e45b794…` and `a9fc7f4b…`; round 7 preserves the safe release rejection through target-level pre-handle cleanup retries | Threads `PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jvje4` and `PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jv3uO`; failed-open OAuth cleanup remains `token-revocation` / `authorization-server` and the retry owner succeeds later |\n| `MCP170-CLEANUP-OWNER` | Retryable suite/test/conformance cleanup errors must retain their owning client/lease through `runCli`; only `bin.ts` may terminate, after a diagnostic is flushed | Fixed locally; recurring family | pre-ledger | round 1 | Prior `1e45b794…` fixed some paths; round 1 persists reports, propagates all owning errors, retries at the library boundary, and flushes executable diagnostics | Four current comments deduplicate to this root cause; CLI regressions prove report persistence and a second caller-owned retry after the bounded retry fails |\n| `MCP170-SNAPSHOT-PAYLOAD-MODE` | Inspector standalone descriptor/server/capability/inventory projections must use payload redaction, not envelope preservation | Fixed locally; recurring credential-boundary sibling | pre-ledger | round 1 | Round 1 passes `preserveKeys: false` for all snapshot projections | Two current comments deduplicate to this root cause; mode-sensitive snapshot regression passes |\n| `MCP170-OAUTH-TOKEN-CACHE` | Successful exchange and refresh responses must have a JSON media type and both mandated non-cache headers | Fixed locally | round 1 | round 1 | Round 1 validates both directives and adds them to hosted and mock producers | Exchange/refresh regressions cover each missing directive; auth and testing suites pass |\n| `MCP170-TEST-…5236 tokens truncated…after: 2 selected code root causes plus 1 deferred analyzer family / 1 deferred analyzer family.\n- New defects: direct structural credential collisions in client/inspector artifacts and lost post-connect test-client cleanup ownership; the Sonar summary also reports one additional unclassified issue.\n- Recurring families: credential-boundary and cleanup-ownership defects both recurred; the reset-trigger subsystem assessment expanded enforcement to their shared producers before mutation.\n- Fix-induced regressions: the first full gate exposed lost post-close omission evidence, which was repaired with shared omission counters; the next gate exposed a mixed-entry test that still required an unsafe fallback envelope, which was updated to assert the counter and empty timeline. The final complete gate found no remaining regression.\n- Oldest deferred blocker: `MCP170-SONAR-MAINTAINABILITY` from round 1, now 36 reported issues. It remains Not Attempted and prevents a clean assessment despite terminal hosted checks.\n- Clean streak: `0` (reset by actionable/new findings, recurring families, this code-changing round, and the changed deferred analyzer inventory).\n- Head stability: the complete snapshot stayed at `9371beeb842106255854ee40f951819fb72ebed8`; the authorized round then advanced local, remote, and PR head together to `afcf360f7bd5bfc348bdf689a7f2b483c1243190` in the one allowed comparison.\n\n## Round 4 verification evidence\n\n- Focused testing: 36 client regressions, 37 inspector regressions, 132 testing-package regressions, and 27 CLI regressions passed.\n- Typechecks: `@mcpfn/client`, `@mcpfn/inspector`, `@mcpfn/testing`, and `@mcpfn/cli` passed.\n- Mixed entrypoint: the ESM-client/CJS-inspector omission-provenance consumer passed with the shared omission counter and no unsafe fallback event.\n- Release gate: the final `PATH=/opt/homebrew/opt/node@22/bin:$PATH npm run gate:mcpfn-release` passed all package tests/builds, Playwright, official conformance, mixed-entry redaction, examples, ESM/CJS imports, package dry-runs, packed-consumer installation, and round trips.\n- `git diff --check`: passed; the whole code/test/documentation batch was reviewed before staging.\n- Hosted CI/reviewer state will not be refetched or polled after this snapshot.\n\n## Round 4 termination\n\n- Result: nonterminal recurring run; automation remains enabled for a future separately scheduled assessment.\n- No post-push review refetch, CI poll, reviewer wait, thread resolution, review dismissal, merge, or second fix loop was performed.\n\n## Round 5 snapshot dispositions\n\n- Actionable at snapshot: 1 new unresolved GitHub thread, deduplicated to 1 root cause. The root cause is fixed in the reviewed local batch; the thread was not resolved and review surfaces will not be refetched after push.\n- Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale): 80 older unresolved threads. Two are round-4 findings already repaired at the assessed head; 78 retain prior current-code evidence because the snapshot head is exactly the round-4 pushed head outside this batch. Outdated status was not used as proof.\n- Invalid / Accepted Risk: 1 older GitHub finding remains invalid based on observed key-redaction behavior; no accepted risks.\n- Not Attempted: the Sonar summary still reports 36 open maintainability/reliability findings although its Quality Gate passed. `MCP170-SONAR-MAINTAINABILITY` remains deferred and visible as one external analyzer family; individual analyzer details were not present in the captured review surfaces.\n- Unassessed: 0 captured GitHub threads, review bodies, issue comments, or inline comments.\n\n## Round 5 required-check snapshot\n\n- One snapshot only: 18 contexts comprised 11 successful, 2 in progress (CodeQL Swift and Pullfrog), 2 neutral, and 3 skipped. SonarCloud and Cubic were successful; `reviewDecision` was empty. No check polling is permitted.\n\n## Round 5 convergence\n\n- Trend: stalled. The one newly discovered code root is repaired locally, but it is another credential-boundary sibling, two hosted checks were pending in the only allowed snapshot, and the 36-item analyzer family remains deferred.\n- Valid blocking root causes before / after: 1 selected code root cause plus 1 deferred analyzer family / 1 deferred analyzer family.\n- New defects: malformed event/diagnostic timestamps after credential redaction.\n- Recurring families: credential-boundary structural safety recurred; the reset-trigger assessment moved enforcement to the shared timestamp producer before mutation.\n- Fix-induced regressions: none found by focused client tests, consumer/package checks, complete diff review, or the Node 22 release gate. One initial focused invocation used unsupported Vitest flag `--runInBand` and failed before collection; the configured package command superseded it and passed.\n- Oldest deferred blocker: `MCP170-SONAR-MAINTAINABILITY` from round 1, still 36 reported issues. It remains Not Attempted and prevents a clean assessment.\n- Clean streak: `0` (reset by an actionable/new finding, a recurring family, this code-changing round, two pending hosted checks, and the deferred analyzer family).\n- Head stability: the complete snapshot stayed at `afcf360f7bd5bfc348bdf689a7f2b483c1243190`; the authorized round then advanced local, remote, and PR head together to `30e7ff8e2c61fb9e872b37ec104278ea0c893a71` in the one allowed comparison.\n\n## Round 5 verification evidence\n\n- Focused testing: all 42 `@mcpfn/client` tests passed. The new exact-collision regression proves emitted omission timestamps remain canonical ISO and credential-free; the no-safe-ISO regression proves malformed artifacts are dropped while both omission counters advance. These assertions distinguish the prior behavior, which emitted redacted non-ISO values.\n- Typecheck: `@mcpfn/client` passed.\n- Release gate: `PATH=/opt/homebrew/opt/node@22/bin:$PATH npm run gate:mcpfn-release` passed all package tests/builds, Playwright, official conformance, mixed-entry redaction, examples, ESM/CJS imports, package dry-runs, packed-consumer installation, and round trips.\n- `git diff --check`: passed; the whole two-file code/test batch and downstream timestamp consumers were reviewed before staging.\n- Hosted CI/reviewer state will not be refetched or polled after this snapshot.\n\n## Round 5 termination\n\n- Result: nonterminal recurring run; automation remains enabled for a future separately scheduled assessment.\n- No post-push review refetch, CI poll, reviewer wait, thread resolution, review dismissal, merge, or second fix loop was performed.\n\n## Round 6 snapshot dispositions\n\n- Actionable at snapshot: 1 new unresolved GitHub thread, deduplicated to the recurring `MCP170-CLEANUP-PHASE` root cause. The root cause is fixed in the reviewed local batch; the thread was not resolved and review surfaces will not be refetched after push.\n- Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale): 80 older unresolved threads retain prior current-code evidence because the assessed head is exactly the round-5 pushed head outside this batch. Outdated status was not used as proof.\n- Invalid / Accepted Risk: 1 older GitHub finding remains invalid based on observed key-redaction behavior; no accepted risks.\n- Not Attempted: the Sonar summary still reports 36 open maintainability/reliability findings although its Quality Gate passed. `MCP170-SONAR-MAINTAINABILITY` remains deferred and visible as one external analyzer family; individual analyzer details were not present in the captured review surfaces.\n- Unassessed: 0 captured GitHub threads, review bodies, issue comments, or inline comments.\n\n## Round 6 required-check snapshot\n\n- One snapshot only: all 18 contexts were terminal: 14 successful, 1 neutral, and 3 skipped. There were no pending or failed contexts; `reviewDecision` was empty. SonarCloud reported a passing Quality Gate and 36 open issues. No check polling is permitted.\n\n## Round 6 convergence\n\n- Trend: stalled. The one selected code root is repaired locally, but cleanup-phase attribution recurred and the preserved 36-item analyzer family remains deferred.\n- Valid blocking root causes before / after: 1 selected code root cause plus 1 deferred analyzer family / 1 deferred analyzer family.\n- New defects: authenticated-target revocation failures were attributed to transport shutdown in direct diagnostics and suite reports.\n- Recurring families: cleanup-phase classification recurred from the historical pre-ledger repair; the reset-trigger assessment extended the phase contract through target, client, retained-owner, and report boundaries before mutation.\n- Fix-induced regressions: the first testing run exposed a stale built-client consumer boundary plus changed stable cleanup wording; after rebuilding, an ownership regression showed failed-connect cleanup had replaced the original connection cause. The batch now retains the phase separately while preserving the original cause and established report message. Final focused suites and the release gate found no remaining regression.\n- Oldest deferred blocker: `MCP170-SONAR-MAINTAINABILITY` from round 1, still 36 reported issues. It remains Not Attempted and prevents a clean assessment.\n- Clean streak: `0` (reset by an actionable/new finding, a recurring family, this code-changing round, and the deferred analyzer family).\n- Head stability: the complete snapshot stayed at `30e7ff8e2c61fb9e872b37ec104278ea0c893a71`; the authorized round then advanced local, remote, and PR head together to `a9fc7f4b1e6b549caf1a5f80498980167429482a` in the one allowed comparison.\n\n## Round 6 verification evidence\n\n- Focused testing: all 42 `@mcpfn/client` tests and all 221 `@mcpfn/testing` tests passed. The new assertions distinguish raw transport failure (`transport-close`), OAuth revoke failure (`token-revocation` / `authorization-server`), and disposal after successful revocation (`transport-close` / `mcp