snapshot.\n\n## Round 4 termination\n\n- Result: nonterminal recurring run; automation remains enabled for a future separately scheduled assessment.\n- No post-push review refetch, CI poll, reviewer wait, thread resolution, review dismissal, merge, or second fix loop was performed.\n\n## Round 5 snapshot dispositions\n\n- Actionable at snapshot: 1 new unresolved GitHub thread, deduplicated to 1 root cause. The root cause is fixed in the reviewed local batch; the thread was not resolved and review surfaces will not be refetched after push.\n- Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale): 80 older unresolved threads. Two are round-4 findings already repaired at the assessed head; 78 retain prior current-code evidence because the snapshot head is exactly the round-4 pushed head outside this batch. Outdated status was not used as proof.\n- Invalid / Accepted Risk: 1 older GitHub finding remains invalid based on observed key-redaction behavior; no accepted risks.\n- Not Attempted: the Sonar summary still reports 36 open maintainability/reliability findings although its Quality Gate passed. `MCP170-SONAR-MAINTAINABILITY` remains deferred and visible as one external analyzer family; individual analyzer details were not present in the captured review surfaces.\n- Unassessed: 0 captured GitHub threads, review bodies, issue comments, or inline comments.\n\n## Round 5 required-check snapshot\n\n- One snapshot only: 18 contexts comprised 11 successful, 2 in progress (CodeQL Swift and Pullfrog), 2 neutral, and 3 skipped. SonarCloud and Cubic were successful; `reviewDecision` was empty. No check polling is permitted.\n\n## Round 5 convergence\n\n- Trend: stalled. The one newly discovered code root is repaired locally, but it is another credential-boundary sibling, two hosted checks were pending in the only allowed snapshot, and the 36-item analyzer family remains deferred.\n- Valid blocking root causes before / after: 1 selected code root cause plus 1 deferred analyzer family / 1 deferred analyzer family.\n- New defects: malformed event/diagnostic timestamps after credential redaction.\n- Recurring families: credential-boundary structural safety recurred; the reset-trigger assessment moved enforcement to the shared timestamp producer before mutation.\n- Fix-induced regressions: none found by focused client tests, consumer/package checks, complete diff review, or the Node 22 release gate. One initial focused invocation used unsupported Vitest flag `--runInBand` and failed before collection; the configured package command superseded it and passed.\n- Oldest deferred blocker: `MCP170-SONAR-MAINTAINABILITY` from round 1, still 36 reported issues. It remains Not Attempted and prevents a clean assessment.\n- Clean streak: `0` (reset by an actionable/new finding, a recurring family, this code-changing round, two pending hosted checks, and the deferred analyzer family).\n- Head stability: the complete snapshot stayed at `afcf360f7bd5bfc348bdf689a7f2b483c1243190`; the authorized round then advanced local, remote, and PR head together to `30e7ff8e2c61fb9e872b37ec104278ea0c893a71` in the one allowed comparison.\n\n## Round 5 verification evidence\n\n- Focused testing: all 42 `@mcpfn/client` tests passed. The new exact-collision regression proves emitted omission timestamps remain canonical ISO and credential-free; the no-safe-ISO regression proves malformed artifacts are dropped while both omission counters advance. These assertions distinguish the prior behavior, which emitted redacted non-ISO values.\n- Typecheck: `@mcpfn/client` passed.\n- Release gate: `PATH=/opt/homebrew/opt/node@22/bin:$PATH npm run gate:mcpfn-release` passed all package tests/builds, Playwright, official conformance, mixed-entry redaction, examples, ESM/CJS imports, package dry-runs, packed-consumer installation, and round trips.\n- `git diff --check`: passed; the whole two-file code/test batch and downstream timestamp consumers were reviewed before staging.\n- Hosted CI/reviewer state will not be refetched or polled after this snapshot.\n\n## Round 5 termination\n\n- Result: nonterminal recurring run; automation remains enabled for a future separately scheduled assessment.\n- No post-push review refetch, CI poll, reviewer wait, thread resolution, review dismissal, merge, or second fix loop was performed.\n\n## Round 6 snapshot dispositions\n\n- Actionable at snapshot: 1 new unresolved GitHub thread, deduplicated to the recurring `MCP170-CLEANUP-PHASE` root cause. The root cause is fixed in the reviewed local batch; the thread was not resolved and review surfaces will not be refetched after push.\n- Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale): 80 older unresolved threads retain prior current-code evidence because the assessed head is exactly the round-5 pushed head outside this batch. Outdated status was not used as proof.\n- Invalid / Accepted Risk: 1 older GitHub finding remains invalid based on observed key-redaction behavior; no accepted risks.\n- Not Attempted: the Sonar summary still reports 36 open maintainability/reliability findings although its Quality Gate passed. `MCP170-SONAR-MAINTAINABILITY` remains deferred and visible as one external analyzer family; individual analyzer details were not present in the captured review surfaces.\n- Unassessed: 0 captured GitHub threads, review bodies, issue comments, or inline comments.\n\n## Round 6 required-check snapshot\n\n- One snapshot only: all 18 contexts were terminal: 14 successful, 1 neutral, and 3 skipped. There were no pending or failed contexts; `reviewDecision` was empty. SonarCloud reported a passing Quality Gate and 36 open issues. No check polling is permitted.\n\n## Round 6 convergence\n\n- Trend: stalled. The one selected code root is repaired locally, but cleanup-phase attribution recurred and the preserved 36-item analyzer family remains deferred.\n- Valid blocking root causes before / after: 1 selected code root cause plus 1 deferred analyzer family / 1 deferred analyzer family.\n- New defects: authenticated-target revocation failures were attributed to transport shutdown in direct diagnostics and suite reports.\n- Recurring families: cleanup-phase classification recurred from the historical pre-ledger repair; the reset-trigger assessment extended the phase contract through target, client, retained-owner, and report boundaries before mutation.\n- Fix-induced regressions: the first testing run exposed a stale built-client consumer boundary plus changed stable cleanup wording; after rebuilding, an ownership regression showed failed-connect cleanup had replaced the original connection cause. The batch now retains the phase separately while preserving the original cause and established report message. Final focused suites and the release gate found no remaining regression.\n- Oldest deferred blocker: `MCP170-SONAR-MAINTAINABILITY` from round 1, still 36 reported issues. It remains Not Attempted and prevents a clean assessment.\n- Clean streak: `0` (reset by an actionable/new finding, a recurring family, this code-changing round, and the deferred analyzer family).\n- Head stability: the complete snapshot stayed at `30e7ff8e2c61fb9e872b37ec104278ea0c893a71`; the authorized round then advanced local, remote, and PR head together to `a9fc7f4b1e6b549caf1a5f80498980167429482a` in the one allowed comparison.\n\n## Round 6 verification evidence\n\n- Focused testing: all 42 `@mcpfn/client` tests and all 221 `@mcpfn/testing` tests passed. The new assertions distinguish raw transport failure (`transport-close`), OAuth revoke failure (`token-revocation` / `authorization-server`), and disposal after successful revocation (`transport-close` / `mcpfn-preflight`) while proving the cleanup owner remains retryable.\n- Typechecks: `@mcpfn/client` and `@mcpfn/testing` passed; the client package was rebuilt before the consumer suite.\n- Release gate: `PATH=/opt/homebrew/opt/node@22/bin:$PATH npm run gate:mcpfn-release` passed all package tests/builds, Playwright, official conformance, mixed-entry redaction, examples, ESM/CJS imports, package dry-runs, packed-consumer installation, and round trips.\n- `git diff --check`: passed; the whole four-file code/test batch and existing conformance/report phase consumers were reviewed before staging.\n- Hosted CI/reviewer state will not be refetched or polled after this snapshot.\n\n## Round 6 termination\n\n- Result: nonterminal recurring run; automation remains enabled for a future separately scheduled assessment.\n- No post-push review refetch, CI poll, reviewer wait, thread resolution, review dismissal, merge, or second fix loop was performed.\n\n## Round 7 snapshot dispositions\n\n- Actionable at snapshot: 5 new unresolved GitHub threads, deduplicated to 4 root causes. Two threads report the same pre-handle cleanup-phase loss; the other roots are cleanup-error sanitization, structured-Cookie redaction, and target-suite runtime-output classification. All four are fixed in the reviewed local batch; threads remain unresolved and will not be refetched after push.\n- Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale): 81 older unresolved threads. The round-6 cleanup-phase thread is already repaired at the assessed head; 80 retain prior current-code evidence. Outdated status was not used as proof.\n- Invalid / Accepted Risk: 1 older GitHub finding remains invalid based on observed key-redaction behavior; no accepted risks.\n- Not Attempted: the preserved Sonar maintainability/reliability inventory remains 36 findings under `MCP170-SONAR-MAINTAINABILITY`. The captured Sonar analysis check is successful, but individual analyzer details were not substituted for the GitHub review snapshot or declared fixed.\n- Unassessed: 0 captured GitHub threads, review bodies, issue comments, or inline comments.\n\n## Round 7 required-check snapshot\n\n- One snapshot only: all 18 contexts were terminal: 14 successful, 1 neutral, and 3 skipped. There were no pending or failed contexts; `reviewDecision` was empty. CodeRabbit, Cubic, Pullfrog, CodeQL, SonarCloud Code Analysis, GitGuardian, and the applicable security checks were successful. No check polling is permitted.\n\n## Round 7 convergence\n\n- Trend: stalled across scheduled convergence. Four selected roots are repaired locally, but cleanup-phase and credential-boundary families recurred again and the 36-finding analyzer family remains deferred.\n- Valid blocking root causes before / after: 4 selected code root causes plus 1 deferred analyzer family / 1 deferred analyzer family.\n- New defects: unsafe cleanup cause exposure, pre-handle phase loss, structured Cookie component leakage, and target-suite output misclassification.\n- Recurring families: cleanup-phase classification and credential-boundary redaction recurred; the reset-trigger assessment repaired their shared lease/target/client boundaries before mutation.\n- Fix-induced regressions: none found by focused regressions, affected-package typechecks/builds, whole-batch diff review, or the final Node 22 release gate.\n- Oldest deferred blocker: `MCP170-SONAR-MAINTAINABILITY` from round 1, last observed at 36 findings. It remains Not Attempted and prevents a clean assessment despite terminal successful required checks.\n- Clean streak: `0` (reset by four actionable/new root causes, recurring families, this code-changing round, and the deferred analyzer family).\n- Head stability: the complete snapshot stayed at `a9fc7f4b1e6b549caf1a5f80498980167429482a`; the authorized round then advanced local, remote, and PR head together to `0e89647cb6a20681e546a22c1d2c58eabe74253e` in the one allowed comparison.\n\n## Round 7 verification evidence\n\n- Focused testing: all 43 `@mcpfn/client` tests, 226 `@mcpfn/testing` tests, and 50 `@mcpfn/cli` tests passed. New regressions prove public cleanup causes cannot expose raw provider errors, failed-open OAuth retries retain `token-revocation`, reflected Cookie components are redacted while ambiguous syntax fails closed, and target JSON/JUnit/stdout failures are safe runtime exit 1 outcomes.\n- Typechecks: `@mcpfn/client`, `@mcpfn/testing`, and `@mcpfn/cli` passed; client and testing packages were rebuilt before downstream consumer tests.\n- Release gate: the final `PATH=/opt/homebrew/opt/node@22/bin:$PATH npm run gate:mcpfn-release` passed all package tests/typechecks/builds, Playwright, official conformance, mixed-entry redaction, examples, ESM/CJS imports, dry-run packs, packed-consumer installation, and round trips.\n- `git diff --check`: passed; the whole six-file code/test batch and affected lease/target/client/suite/CLI consumers were reviewed before staging.\n- Hosted CI/reviewer state will not be refetched or polled after this snapshot.\n\n## Round 7 termination\n\n- Result: nonterminal recurring run; automation remains enabled for a future separately scheduled assessment.\n- No post-push review refetch, CI poll, reviewer wait, thread resolution, review dismissal, merge, or second fix loop was performed.\n\n## Round 8 snapshot dispositions\n\n- Actionable at snapshot: 2 new unresolved GitHub threads, deduplicated to 2 root causes. Both are fixed in the reviewed local batch; threads remain unresolved and will not be refetched after push.\n- Skipped (Already Fixed / Stale): 83 older unresolved threads. The five round-7 findings are already repaired at the assessed head; 78 retain earlier current-code evidence. Outdated status was not used as proof.\n- Invalid / Accepted Risk: 1 older GitHub finding remains invalid based on observed key-redaction behavior; no accepted risks.\n- Not Attempted: the preserved Sonar maintainability/reliability inventory remains 36 findings under `MCP170-SONAR-MAINTAINABILITY`. The captured Sonar analysis check is successful, but individual analyzer details were not substituted for the GitHub review snapshot or declared fixed.\n- Unassessed: 0 captured GitHub threads, review bodies, issue comments, or inline comments.\n\n## Round 8 required-check snapshot\n\n- One snapshot only: 18 contexts comprised 12 successful, 1 in progress (`Analyze (swift)`), 2 neutral, and 3 skipped. CodeRabbit, GitGuardian, SonarCloud, Cubic, Pullfrog, and applicable completed security checks were successful; `reviewDecision` was empty. No check polling is permitted.\n\n## Round 8 convergence\n\n- Trend: stalled across scheduled convergence. Both selected roots are repaired locally, but credential-boundary and CLI runtime-output families recurred, one hosted check was pending in the only allowed snapshot, and the 36-finding analyzer family remains deferred.\n- Valid blocking root causes before / after: 2 selected code root causes plus 1 deferred analyzer family / 1 deferred analyzer family.\n- New defects: raw custom-target kind reconstruction after redaction and conformance output/persistence usage-error classification.\n- Recurring families: credential-boundary structural safety and CLI runtime-output classification recurred; the reset-trigger assessment reused their shared client structural validator and cleanup-aware output boundary before mutation.\n- Fix-induced regressions: the first full gate exposed changed incomplete-reason wording in three existing structural-projection regressions. The batch now preserves both the established credential-redaction and safe-serialization semantics; 119 focused testing regressions and the final complete gate pass.\n- Oldest deferred blocker: `MCP170-SONAR-MAINTAINABILITY` from round 1, last observed at 36 findings. It remains Not Attempted and prevents a clean assessment.\n- Clean streak: `0` (reset by two actionable/new root causes, recurring families, this code-changing round, one pending hosted check, and the deferred analyzer family).\n- Head stability: the complete snapshot stayed at `0e89647cb6a20681e546a22c1d2c58eabe74253e`; the authorized round then advanced local, remote, and PR head together to `dfaeef0d1a4b25cc2ae9e10ccde0481b255637ad` in the one allowed comparison.\n\n## Round 8 verification evidence\n\n- Focused testing: 119 target-suite/remote-target regressions and 15 conformance-cleanup regressions passed. New assertions prove a custom target kind that conflicts with its redactor cannot enter JSON/JUnit artifacts and conformance report/stdout/stderr failures return exit 1 with fixed safe text.\n- Typechecks/builds: `@mcpfn/testing` and `@mcpfn/cli` passed under Node 22 before the complete gate.\n- Release gate: the final `PATH=/opt/homebrew/opt/node@22/bin:$PATH npm run gate:mcpfn-release` passed all package tests/typechecks/builds, Playwright, official conformance, mixed-entry redaction, examples, ESM/CJS imports, dry-run packs, packed-consumer installation, and round trips.\n- `git diff --check`: passed; the whole four-file code/test batch and affected suite/CLI consumers were reviewed before staging.\n- Hosted CI/reviewer state will not be refetched or polled after this snapshot.\n\n## Round 8 termination\n\n- Result: nonterminal recurring run; automation remains enabled for a future separately scheduled assessment.\n- No post-push review refetch, CI poll, reviewer wait, thread resolution, review dismissal, merge, or second fix loop was performed.\n\n## Round 1 termination\n\n- Result: nonterminal recurring run; automation remains enabled for a future separately scheduled assessment.\n- No post-push review refetch, CI poll, reviewer wait, thread resolution, review dismissal, merge, or second fix loop was performed.\n\n## Historical handoff retained\n\n- Earlier bounded remediation produced `1e45b794d946722409dc86681a1ee9a34bfe7f2f` for credential redaction, cleanup-phase tracking, cleanup-path tests, and safe CLI handling.\n- At that round's one allowed immediate comparison, the remote branch matched the new commit while the PR API still reported the prior head `8b23aaa087b60844c63459a1c625a23dc954ba03`; this round must establish current live state independently.\n","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aad4e74265c87d0982ca7ea70b5b383","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"summary":[{"text":"**Planning snapshot script using temporary directory**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Designing GitHub API pagination strategy**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Clarifying constraints on temporary file creation**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aad4e7dd23887d0a861a8815e678dc1","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"summary":[{"text":"**Designing parallel CLI execution helper**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Planning GraphQL pagination and parsing**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Assessing PR metadata and comment APIs**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aad4e8728e487d0a9ccc06ee163fc2f","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"summary":[{"text":"**Designing thread comments pagination strategy**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Planning unresolved threads filtering and snapshot storage**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Implementing paginated comment retrieval**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"call_id":"call_KMSIMF7qYtkwL7odafuERE6C","id":"ctc_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aad4e8f963487d0882aa428148babf8","input":"const shq = s =\u003e \"'\" + String(s).replaceAll(\"'\", \"'\\\"'\\\"'\") + \"'\";\nconst gh = async (args, max=100000) =\u003e {\n  const r = await tools.exec_command({\n    cmd: `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other gh ${args}`,\n    workdir: \"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\",\n    yield_time_ms: 30000,\n    max_output_tokens: max\n  });\n  if (r.exit_code !== 0) throw new Error(r.output);\n  return JSON.parse(r.output);\n};\nconst threadQuery = `query($endCursor: String) {\n  repository(owner: \"21nCo\", name: \"super-functions\") {\n    pullRequest(number: 170) {\n      headRefOid state headRefName\n      reviewThreads(first: 100, after: $endCursor) {\n        nodes {\n          id isResolved path line originalLine\n          comments(first: 100) {\n            nodes { id body author { login } createdAt updatedAt outdated url diffHunk originalLine line }\n            pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\n          }\n        }\n        pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}`;\nconst reviewQuery = `query($endCursor: String) {\n  repository(owner: \"21nCo\", name: \"super-functions\") {\n    pullRequest(number: 170) {\n      headRefOid state headRefName\n      reviews(first: 100, after: $endCursor) {\n        nodes { id body state submittedAt url author { login } commit { oid } }\n        pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}`;\nasync function collectConnection(query, key) {\n  let cursor = null, pages = 0, nodes = [], heads = [], nestedIncomplete = [];\n  for (;;) {\n    const cursorArg = cursor ? ` -F endCursor=${shq(cursor)}` : \"\";\n    const data = await gh(`api graphql -f query=${shq(query)}${cursorArg}`, 160000);\n    const pr = data.data.repository.pullRequest;\n    heads.push(pr.headRefOid);\n    const conn = pr[key];\n    nodes.push(...conn.nodes);\n    pages++;\n    if (key === \"reviewThreads\") {\n      for (const t of conn.nodes) if (t.comments.pageInfo.hasNextPage) nestedIncomplete.push(t.id);\n    }\n    if (!conn.pageInfo.hasNextPage) return {nodes,pages,heads,nestedIncomplete,terminal:true};\n    cursor = conn.pageInfo.endCursor;\n    if (!cursor || pages \u003e 20) throw new Error(`${key} pagination failed`);\n  }\n}\nasync function collectRest(path) {\n  let page=1, pages=0, nodes=[];\n  for (;;) {\n    const data = await gh(`api ${shq(`${path}${path.includes(\"?\") ? \"\u0026\" : \"?\"}per_page=100\u0026page=${page}`)}`, 160000);\n    if (!Array.isArray(data)) throw new Error(`REST shape ${path}`);\n    nodes.push(...data); pages++;\n    if (data.length \u003c 100) return {nodes,pages,terminal:true};\n    page++;\n    if (page \u003e 20) throw new Error(`REST pagination failed ${path}`);\n  }\n}\nconst [threads, reviews, issueComments, inlineComments, meta] = await Promise.all([\n  collectConnection(threadQuery, \"reviewThreads\"),\n  collectConnection(reviewQuery, \"reviews\"),\n  collectRest(\"repos/21nCo/super-functions/issues/170/comments\"),\n  collectRest(\"repos/21nCo/super-functions/pulls/170/comments\"),\n  gh(\"pr view 170 --repo 21nCo/super-functions --json headRefOid,headRefName,baseRefName,state,mergeStateStatus,reviewDecision,url,updatedAt,statusCheckRollup\", 80000)\n]);\nconst allHeads = [...threads.heads, ...reviews.heads, meta.headRefOid];\nconst stableHead = allHeads.every(x =\u003e x === allHeads[0]);\nconst unresolved = threads.nodes.filter(t =\u003e !t.isResolved);\nconst current = unresolved.filter(t =\u003e t.comments.nodes.some(c =\u003e !c.outdated));\nconst outdatedOnly = unresolved.filter(t =\u003e t.comments.nodes.every(c =\u003e c.outdated));\nconst checks = meta.statusCheckRollup ?? [];\nconst checkSummary = {};\nfor (const c of checks) {\n  const k = c.__typename === \"CheckRun\" ? (c.conclusion || c.status || \"UNKNOWN\") : (c.state || \"UNKNOWN\");\n  checkSummary[k] = (checkSummary[k] || 0) + 1;\n}\nconst headTimeRes = await tools.exec_command({\n  cmd: \"git show -s --format=%cI HEAD\",\n  workdir: \"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\",\n  yield_time_ms: 10000,\n  max_output_tokens: 1000\n});\nconst headTime = headTimeRes.output.trim();\nconst recentCurrent = current.map(t =\u003e {\n  const cs = t.comments.nodes.filter(c =\u003e !c.outdated);\n  const last = cs.at(-1) || t.comments.nodes.at(-1);\n  return {\n    id:t.id,path:t.path,line:t.line,originalLine:t.originalLine,\n    comments:t.comments.nodes.length,\n    lastCreatedAt:last?.createdAt,author:last?.author?.login,url:last?.url,\n    body:(last?.body||\"\").slice(0,2000)\n  };\n}).sort((a,b)=\u003eString(a.lastCreatedAt).localeCompare(String(b.lastCreatedAt)));\nconst snapshot = {\n  capturedAt:new Date().toISOString(), meta, threads, reviews, issueComments, inlineComments,\n  stableHead, allHeads, unresolvedCount:unresolved.length, currentCount:current.length,\n  outdatedOnlyCount:outdatedOnly.length, checks, checkSummary, headTime, recentCurrent\n};\nstore(\"pr170_round9_snapshot\", snapshot);\ntext(JSON.stringify({\n  capturedAt:snapshot.capturedAt,\n  head:meta.headRefOid,\n  state:meta.state,\n  stableHead,\n  pages:{threads:threads.pages,reviews:reviews.pages,issueComments:issueComments.pages,inlineComments:inlineComments.pages},\n  counts:{threads:threads.nodes.length,unresolved:unresolved.length,current:current.length,outdatedOnly:outdatedOnly.length,reviews:reviews.nodes.length,issueComments:issueComments.nodes.length,inlineComments:inlineComments.nodes.length,checks:checks.length},\n  nestedIncomplete:threads.nestedIncomplete,\n  checks:checkSummary,\n  reviewDecision:meta.reviewDecision,\n  mergeStateStatus:meta.mergeStateStatus,\n  headTime,\n  currentThreads:recentCurrent\n}, null, 2));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789742707.231587,"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_KMSIMF7qYtkwL7odafuERE6C","id":"ctco_01a0b4fb-4933-77e3-b233-79447147b070","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789742762.291537,"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"output":[{"text":"Script failed\nWall time 1.5 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"Script error:\nSyntaxError: Unexpected token 'W', \"Warning: t\"... is not valid JSON\n    at JSON.parse (\u003canonymous\u003e)\n    at gh (exec_main.mjs:10:15)\n    at async collectRest (exec_main.mjs:61:18)\n    at async Promise.all (index 3)\n    at async exec_main.mjs:69:65","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aad4ead050487d09ff16382afe4b650","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"summary":[{"text":"**Handling partial output truncation**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Evaluating snapshot retry versus stopping**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Planning snapshot retry with smaller pages**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aad4eb7964c87d0b3f3d94d3e40adbe","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"summary":[{"text":"**Planning robust JSON parsing with pagination**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"content":[{"text":"The first collector attempt did not establish a snapshot: one large raw-inline page was truncated by the command transport before JSON parsing, so nothing was stored or assessed. I’m retrying the pre-edit collection with smaller pages and explicit head consistency checks; this is recovery of the failed initial collection, not a second review round, and no edits have occurred.","type":"output_text"}],"id":"msg_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aad4ebc5ad487d09be70e0b587f601c","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["unknown"],"create_time":1789742763.815372,"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"phase":"commentary","role":"assistant","type":"message"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aad4ebca6e087d08b193ce381d08dc5","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"summary":[{"text":"**Planning efficient GraphQL data fetching**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Designing concurrent call handling**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"call_id":"call_UmbiIDI0DGDODHq0rKbFPGjU","id":"ctc_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aad4ec0c1e887d0b640aecdc19f6278","input":"const shq = s =\u003e \"'\" + String(s).replaceAll(\"'\", \"'\\\"'\\\"'\") + \"'\";\nconst parseJson = out =\u003e {\n  const starts = [out.indexOf(\"{\"), out.indexOf(\"[\")].filter(i =\u003e i \u003e= 0);\n  if (!starts.length) throw new Error(`No JSON in output: ${out.slice(0,300)}`);\n  const start = Math.min(...starts);\n  return JSON.parse(out.slice(start));\n};\nconst gh = async (args, max=80000) =\u003e {\n  const r = await tools.exec_command({\n    cmd: `GH_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/serro/.config/gh-other gh ${args}`,\n    workdir: \"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\",\n    yield_time_ms: 30000,\n    max_output_tokens: max\n  });\n  if (r.exit_code !== 0) throw new Error(r.output);\n  return parseJson(r.output);\n};\nconst threadQuery = `query($endCursor: String) {\n  repository(owner: \"21nCo\", name: \"super-functions\") {\n    pullRequest(number: 170) {\n      headRefOid state headRefName\n      reviewThreads(first: 30, after: $endCursor) {\n        nodes {\n          id isResolved path line originalLine\n          comments(first: 100) {\n            nodes { id body author { login } createdAt updatedAt outdated url diffHunk originalLine line }\n            pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\n          }\n        }\n        pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}`;\nconst reviewQuery = `query($endCursor: String) {\n  repository(owner: \"21nCo\", name: \"super-functions\") {\n    pullRequest(number: 170) {\n      headRefOid state headRefName\n      reviews(first: 50, after: $endCursor) {\n        nodes { id body state submittedAt url author { login } commit { oid } }\n        pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}`;\nasync function collectConnection(query, key) {\n  let cursor = null, pages = 0, nodes = [], heads = [], nestedIncomplete = [];\n  for (;;) {\n    const cursorArg = cursor ? ` -F endCursor=${shq(cursor)}` : \"\";\n    const data = await gh(`api graphql -f query=${shq(query)}${cursorArg}`);\n    const pr = data.data.repository.pullRequest;\n    heads.push(pr.headRefOid);\n    const conn = pr[key];\n    nodes.push(...conn.nodes);\n    pages++;\n    if (key === \"reviewThreads\") {\n      for (const t of conn.nodes) if (t.comments.pageInfo.hasNextPage) nestedIncomplete.push(t.id);\n    }\n    if (!conn.pageInfo.hasNextPage) return {nodes,pages,heads,nestedIncomplete,terminal:true};\n    cursor = conn.pageInfo.endCursor;\n    if (!cursor || pages \u003e 30) throw new Error(`${key} pagination failed`);\n  }\n}\nasync function collectRest(path, perPage) {\n  let page=1, pages=0, nodes=[];\n  for (;;) {\n    const data = await gh(`api ${shq(`${path}${path.includes(\"?\") ? \"\u0026\" : \"?\"}per_page=${perPage}\u0026page=${page}`)}`);\n    if (!Array.isArray(data)) throw new Error(`REST shape ${path}`);\n    nodes.push(...data); pages++;\n    if (data.length \u003c perPage) return {nodes,pages,terminal:true};\n    page++;\n    if (page \u003e 40) throw new Error(`REST pagination failed ${path}`);\n  }\n}\nconst threadsP = collectConnection(threadQuery, \"reviewThreads\");\nconst reviewsP = collectConnection(reviewQuery, \"reviews\");\nconst issuesP = collectRest(\"repos/21nCo/super-functions/issues/170/comments\", 50);\nconst inlineP = collectRest(\"repos/21nCo/super-functions/pulls/170/comments\", 25);\nconst metaP = gh(\"pr view 170 --repo 21nCo/super-functions --json headRefOid,headRefName,baseRefName,state,mergeStateStatus,reviewDecision,url,updatedAt,statusCheckRollup\");\nconst [threads, reviews, issueComments, inlineComments, meta] = await Promise.all([threadsP,reviewsP,issuesP,inlineP,metaP]);\nconst allHeads = [...threads.heads, ...reviews.heads, meta.headRefOid];\nconst stableHead = allHeads.every(x =\u003e x === allHeads[0]);\nconst unresolved = threads.nodes.filter(t =\u003e !t.isResolved);\nconst current = unresolved.filter(t =\u003e t.comments.nodes.some(c =\u003e !c.outdated));\nconst outdatedOnly = unresolved.filter(t =\u003e t.comments.nodes.every(c =\u003e c.outdated));\nconst checks = meta.statusCheckRollup ?? [];\nconst checkSummary = {};\nfor (const c of checks) {\n  const k = c.__typename === \"CheckRun\" ? (c.conclusion || c.status || \"UNKNOWN\") : (c.state || \"UNKNOWN\");\n  checkSummary[k] = (checkSummary[k] || 0) + 1;\n}\nconst headTimeRes = await tools.exec_command({\n  cmd: \"git show -s --format=%cI HEAD\",\n  workdir: \"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\",\n  yield_time_ms: 10000,\n  max_output_tokens: 1000\n});\nconst headTime = headTimeRes.output.trim();\nconst currentThreads = current.map(t =\u003e {\n  const cs = t.comments.nodes.filter(c =\u003e !c.outdated);\n  const last = cs.at(-1) || t.comments.nodes.at(-1);\n  return {\n    id:t.id,path:t.path,line:t.line,originalLine:t.originalLine,\n    comments:t.comments.nodes.length,\n    lastCreatedAt:last?.createdAt,author:last?.author?.login,url:last?.url,\n    body:(last?.body||\"\").slice(0,2500)\n  };\n}).sort((a,b)=\u003eString(a.lastCreatedAt).localeCompare(String(b.lastCreatedAt)));\nconst snapshot = {\n  capturedAt:new Date().toISOString(), meta, threads, reviews, issueComments, inlineComments,\n  stableHead, allHeads, unresolvedCount:unresolved.length, currentCount:current.length,\n  outdatedOnlyCount:outdatedOnly.length, checks, checkSummary, headTime, currentThreads\n};\nstore(\"pr170_round9_snapshot\", snapshot);\ntext(JSON.stringify({\n  capturedAt:snapshot.capturedAt,\n  head:meta.headRefOid,state:meta.state,stableHead,\n  pages:{threads:threads.pages,reviews:reviews.pages,issueComments:issueComments.pages,inlineComments:inlineComments.pages},\n  counts:{threads:threads.nodes.length,unresolved:unresolved.length,current:current.length,outdatedOnly:outdatedOnly.length,reviews:reviews.nodes.length,issueComments:issueComments.nodes.length,inlineComments:inlineComments.nodes.length,checks:checks.length},\n  nestedIncomplete:threads.nestedIncomplete,checks:checkSummary,\n  reviewDecision:meta.reviewDecision,mergeStateStatus:meta.mergeStateStatus,\n  headTime,currentThreads\n}, null, 2));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789742763.815372,"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_UmbiIDI0DGDODHq0rKbFPGjU","id":"ctco_01a0b4fc-4726-74c0-a1d7-a3afbfcc84af","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789742827.302299,"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 17.8 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 11913)\nTotal output lines: 462\n\n{\n  \"capturedAt\": \"2026-09-18T14:47:07.269Z\",\n  \"head\": \"dfaeef0d1a4b25cc2ae9e10ccde0481b255637ad\",\n  \"state\": \"OPEN\",\n  \"stableHead\": true,\n  \"pages\": {\n    \"threads\": 11,\n    \"reviews\": 5,\n    \"issueComments\": 2,\n    \"inlineComments\": 15\n  },\n  \"counts\": {\n    \"threads\": 325,\n    \"unresolved\": 90,\n    \"current\": 39,\n    \"outdatedOnly\": 51,\n    \"reviews\": 220,\n    \"issueComments\": 64,\n    \"inlineComments\": 364,\n    \"checks\": 18\n  },\n  \"nestedIncomplete\": [],\n  \"checks\": {\n    \"SUCCESS\": 14,\n    \"NEUTRAL\": 1,\n    \"SKIPPED\": 3\n  },\n  \"reviewDecision\": \"\",\n  \"mergeStateStatus\": \"DIRTY\",\n  \"headTime\": \"2026-09-18T19:11:07+05:30\",\n  \"currentThreads\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6hIm1W\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/testing/src/reports.ts\",\n      \"line\": 119,\n      \"originalLine\": 112,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-10T15:21:07Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r3980654436\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Represent incomplete suites as JUnit failures**\\n\\nFor a suite containing only incomplete scenarios, failure elements are emitted for those scenarios but the `testsuite` attribute still declares `failures=\\\"0\\\"` because `safe.incomplete` is omitted here. A suite made incomplete solely by dropped diagnostic events or report truncation has the same zero-failure declaration and may not contain any failure element at all, so JUnit consumers can display a green run while the JSON report has `ok: false`.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6hIm1d\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/testing/src/suite.ts\",\n      \"line\": 212,\n      \"originalLine\": 108,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-10T15:21:08Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r3980654448\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Enforce visible tools without requiring a manifest**\\n\\nWhen `mcpfn test-target` is invoked with `--visible-tools` but without the separately optional `--manifest`, the CLI forwards the expected names, but this condition skips the only call that uses them. The command can therefore exit successfully against a target exposing the wrong tool inventory while silently ignoring an explicitly requested check; either compare the live names independently or reject this option combination.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6hJBPf\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/testing/src/conformance.ts\",\n      \"line\": 384,\n      \"originalLine\": 317,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-10T15:38:34Z\",\n      \"author\": \"cubic-dev-ai\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r3980817019\",\n      \"body\": \"\u003c!-- cubic:v=af62c06d-a138-4c4d-9e49-6f4e5a3f5aa8 --\u003e\\n\u003c!-- cubic:review-run=2794c884-4528-5630-ad81-9c892ab7aad2 --\u003e\\n\u003c!-- metadata:{\\\"confidence\\\":5} --\u003e\\nP2: When a library caller acquires credentials from an env var and calls runAuthenticatedOfficialConformance without setting sensitiveEnvironmentVariables (defaults to []), the spawned @modelcontextprotocol/conformance npx process inherits that env var, since buildOfficialConformanceEnvironment only strips explicitly listed names. The CLI wires this correctly, but the library API offers no automatic guard. Note in the credential/headers doc that the credential env must also be listed in sensitiveEnvironmentVariables, or strip credential-source env names automatically.\\n\\n\u003cdetails\u003e\\n\u003csummary\u003ePrompt for AI agents\u003c/summary\u003e\\n\\n```text\\nCheck if this issue is valid — if so, understand the root cause and fix it. At mcpfn/testing/src/conformance.ts, line 317:\\n\\n\u003ccomment\u003eWhen a library caller acquires credentials from an env var and calls runAuthenticatedOfficialConformance without setting sensitiveEnvironmentVariables (defaults to []), the spawned @modelcontextprotocol/conformance npx process inherits that env var, since buildOfficialConformanceEnvironment only strips explicitly listed names. The CLI wires this correctly, but the library API offers no automatic guard. Note in the credential/headers doc that the credential env must also be listed in sensitiveEnvironmentVariables, or strip credential-source env names automatically.\u003c/comment\u003e\\n\\n\u003cfile context\u003e\\n@@ -238,14 +310,29 @@ export async function runOfficialConformance(\\n+  if ((headers === undefined) === (credential === undefined)) {\\n+    throw new TypeError(\\\"Provide exactly one of credential or headers for authenticated conformance\\\");\\n+  }\\n+  const lease = await acquireRemoteCredential(\\n+    credential ?? { headers: headers! },\\n+    {\\n\u003c/file context\u003e\\n```\\n\\n\u003c/details\u003e\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6hds9u\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/testing/src/remote-target.ts\",\n      \"line\": 488,\n      \"originalLine\": 127,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-11T12:22:45Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r3989065994\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Redact acquired credential values from artifacts**\\n\\nWhen an authenticated target reflects an opaque credential under a non-sensitive field and a scenario assertion fails, the actual response is embedded in the scenario error, but the acquired header values are never supplied to the redactor. For example, an API key returned as `{ \\\"echo\\\": \\\"opaque-123\\\" }` survives `redactOAuthValue` and is subsequently written to the JSON/JUnit output and CI artifacts. Track the acquired credential values and replace them explicitly before producing diagnostics or reports.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6hds9y\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/testing/src/host-authorization.ts\",\n      \"line\": 245,\n      \"originalLine\": 242,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-11T12:22:45Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r3989066000\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Validate OAuth error responses before accepting rejections**\\n\\nFor expected-rejection fixtures, any response containing an OAuth `error` is accepted before its transport envelope is validated. Consequently, `chatgpt-unregistered-redirect` can pass when the server redirects to the unregistered drifted URI or returns a mismatched state with `error=invalid_request`, and the unsupported-grant case can pass with an HTTP 200 JSON error. Validate the phase-appropriate status and, for authorization redirects, the callback destination and state before assessing the error code.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6hi7n2\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/cli/src/index.ts\",\n      \"line\": 314,\n      \"originalLine\": 229,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-11T16:10:35Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r3991101834\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Scrub acquired credentials from inspect snapshots**\\n\\nWhen the new authenticated `inspect` option is used against a server that reflects the opaque API key or bearer token in initialization metadata or inventory (for example, a tool description), `McpFnInspector.snapshot()` only performs credential-pattern redaction and the raw acquired value remains in stdout and `--output`. The target-suite path explicitly applies `redactTargetCredentials`, but this inspect path does not, so the option advertised as producing a redacted snapshot can leak its credential.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6hvRyg\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/client/src/client.ts\",\n      \"line\": 1174,\n      \"originalLine\": 868,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-12T10:15:58Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r3995915243\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Retain handles whose credential cleanup fails**\\n\\nWhen an authenticated target's protocol closes remotely, or an in-progress open is aborted, the client calls this helper with `strict=false` and then discards the only handle reference. If the credential provider's `revoke` or `dispose` rejects, this catch silently consumes the failure; despite the authenticated handle resetting its close promise for a retry, no later `client.close()` can reach it, and the remote-close path even reports cleanup as succeeded. Preserve the handle for retry or retry credential cleanup before swallowing the transport-close error.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6hwDvL\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/client/src/client.ts\",\n      \"line\": 108,\n      \"originalLine\": 77,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-12T12:36:08Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r3996211859\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Version the modified client before publishing consumers**\\n\\nWhen this change is released, the new cleanup-retention behavior here will not reach registry users because `mcpfn/client/package.json` remains at `0.0.3`, while `@mcpfn/testing@0.0.5` and `@mcpfn/cli@0.0.6` still pin `@mcpfn/client` to that already-published version. The publish workflow installs dependencies from the registry, so released authenticated suites will run against the previous client implementation rather than these changes; bump the client version and update the consumer dependency pins.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6hyYN4\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/client/package.json\",\n      \"line\": 3,\n      \"originalLine\": 3,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-12T18:14:31Z\",\n      \"author\": \"cubic-dev-ai\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r3997111964\",\n      \"body\": \"\u003c!-- cubic:v=19571ced-3947-4690-a694-33cf0f24bb87 --\u003e\\n\u003c!-- cubic:review-run=4e2c1d2d-c516-5a44-9510-21b8f7b1be54 --\u003e\\n\u003c!-- metadata:{\\\"confidence\\\":7} --\u003e\\nP2: Bumping @mcpfn/client to 0.0.4 leaves @mcpfn/inspector's dependency on \\\"@mcpfn/client\\\": \\\"0.0.3\\\" unsatisfied by the workspace (which is now 0.0.4). With npm workspaces an exact pin is not matched by the higher workspace version, so npm installs a separate copy of the old published client from the registry instead of linking the local 0.0.4 code — exactly the split the lockfile already shows for @mcpfn/testing: mcpfn/inspector/node_modules/@mcpfn/testing and mcpfn/datafn/node_modules/@mcpfn/testing are nested registry installs of testing 0.0.4 (resolved to registry.npmjs.org) while the workspace testing is 0.0.5. Inspector then builds/tests against the pre-fix client 0.0.3, silently missing this PR's cleanup/redaction fixes. Bump the dependent workspace packages in lockstep: @mcpfn/inspector to @mcpfn/client 0.0.4 (and @mcpfn/testing 0.0.5), and @mcpfn/datafn to @mcpfn/testing 0.0.5.\\n\\n\u003cdetails\u003e\\n\u003csummary\u003ePrompt for AI agents\u003c/summary\u003e\\n\\n```text\\nCheck if this issue is valid — if so, understand the root cause and fix it. At mcpfn/client/package.json, line 3:\\n\\n\u003ccomment\u003eBumping @mcpfn/client to 0.0.4 leaves @mcpfn/inspector's dependency on \\\"@mcpfn/client\\\": \\\"0.0.3\\\" unsatisfied by the workspace (which is now 0.0.4). With npm workspaces an exact pin is not matched by the higher workspace version, so npm installs a separate copy of the old published client from the registry instead of linking the local 0.0.4 code — exactly the split the lockfile already shows for @mcpfn/testing: mcpfn/inspector/node_modules/@mcpfn/testing and mcpfn/datafn/node_modules/@mcpfn/testing are nested registry installs of testing 0.0.4 (resolved to registry.npmjs.org) while the workspace testing is 0.0.5. Inspector then builds/tests against the pre-fix client 0.0.3, silently missing this PR's cleanup/redaction fixes. Bump the dependent workspace packages in lockstep: @mcpfn/inspector to @mcpfn/client 0.0.4 (and @mcpfn/testing 0.0.5), and @mcpfn/datafn to @mcpfn/testing 0.0.5.\u003c/comment\u003e\\n\\n\u003cfile context\u003e\\n@@ -1,6 +1,6 @@\\n {\\n   \\\"name\\\": \\\"@mcpfn/client\\\",\\n-  \\\"version\\\": \\\"0.0.3\\\",\\n+  \\\"version\\\": \\\"0.0.4\\\",\\n   \\\"description\\\": \\\"Production MCP client, target, session, lifecycle, and diagnostic engine for McpFn\\\",\\n   \\\"type\\\": \\\"module\\\",\\n\u003c/file context\u003e\\n```\\n\\n\u003c/details\u003e\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6hyYxS\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/testing/src/host-authorization.ts\",\n      \"line\": 453,\n      \"originalLine\": 415,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-12T18:16:03Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r3997115544\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Require JSON media types for successful token responses**\\n\\nFor either authorization-code exchange or refresh, a server can return HTTP 200 with `Content-Type: text/plain` and a JSON-looking body; `Response.json()` still parses it, so the hosted case is reported as passed despite the invalid OAuth token-response envelope. Apply the existing JSON media-type validation to successful token responses before accepting their fields.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6hytBi\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/testing/src/remote-target.ts\",\n      \"line\": 505,\n      \"originalLine\": 280,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-12T19:05:14Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r3997239946\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Retain every lease until cleanup succeeds**\\n\\nWhen an authenticated open succeeds but MCP initialization subsequently fails, this catch is never entered, so the lease is absent from `pendingReleases`. `McpFnTestClient.connectTarget()` can then retry `session.close()` once, swallow a second revocation failure, and discard the session containing the retained handle; even `target.cleanup()` has no reference to that lease, leaving a credential that would succeed on a later retry active indefinitely. Register each acquired lease as pending immediately and remove it only after successful release.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6h2blp\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/testing/src/suite.ts\",\n      \"line\": 348,\n      \"originalLine\": 188,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-13T04:30:51Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r3998727552\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Bound failure text before enforcing the report cap**\\n\\nWhen a target or manifest operation throws a long message and the caller selects a supported small `maxReportBytes` value, the message is copied both here and into `failure.message`. `enforceReportCap()` drops results, timeline, metadata, and failure details, but never truncates either message, so a target throwing roughly 2 KiB of text with a 1024-byte cap makes `runMcpFnTargetSuite()` throw instead of returning a bounded incomplete report. Truncate or omit failure text during cap enforcement.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6h4C83\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/cli/src/index.ts\",\n      \"line\": 571,\n      \"originalLine\": 420,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-13T10:15:34Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r3999354703\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Validate bearer headers without echoing their values**\\n\\nWhen `--bearer-token-env` contains an invalid HTTP header value, such as a multiline token, this branch accepts it without the safe validation used for API keys. Authenticated conformance later constructs `Headers`, whose thrown error includes the offending `Bearer \u003csecret\u003e` value; the conformance cleanup rethrows it and the CLI prints it verbatim to stderr. Validate bearer headers here and replace validation failures with a generic message, or redact setup errors before they escape.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6h7BuQ\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/testing/src/conformance.ts\",\n      \"line\": 401,\n      \"originalLine\": 353,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-13T18:39:15Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r4000501850\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Redact conformance credentials before truncating output**\\n\\nFor authenticated conformance, `runOfficialConformance()` first truncates stdout and stderr to 262,144 characters, and only afterward does this call scrub the acquired credential. If an opaque credential crosses that truncation boundary, only its prefix remains, so exact-value replacement cannot recognize it and the prefix is returned in the report and printed by the CLI. Apply known-credential redaction to raw captured output before any truncation.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6h7UaX\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/testing/src/host-authorization.ts\",\n      \"line\": 382,\n      \"originalLine\": 375,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-13T19:25:10Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r4000615519\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Reject invalid-client authorization redirects**\\n\\nWhen an authorization server redirects to a registered callback with `error=invalid_client` and the correct state, this branch accepts the envelope and the `incompatible-registration-grants` fixture passes. Invalid-client failures must not redirect, as the non-redirect branch itself notes. The fresh evidence beyond the earlier envelope issue is that `validateOAuthRejection` still does not receive or inspect the extracted error code before accepting redirects.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6h7n7g\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/client/src/client.ts\",\n      \"line\": 526,\n      \"originalLine\": 433,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-13T20:06:03Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r4000737352\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Preserve permanent close requests during remote shutdown**\\n\\nWhen the protocol closes remotely and an application calls the default `close()` while this automatic `close(false)` is still running, `close()` returns the existing non-permanent promise, whose completion sets the client state to `idle` rather than `closed`. Thus an explicit permanent shutdown can report success without producing the requested final state; record or escalate the permanent request when sharing the in-flight c…1913 tokens truncated…ts and inspector artifacts. The new generic scheme extraction covers custom schemes but omits Basic's decoded components; decode valid Basic tokens and register both components as secrets.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6iD65P\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/testing/src/conformance.ts\",\n      \"line\": 424,\n      \"originalLine\": 411,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-14T09:52:32Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r4004012227\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Sanitize conformance setup failures before rethrowing**\\n\\nWhen a programmatic credential provider returns an invalid header value, such as one containing a newline, proxy setup throws a `Headers` validation error that includes the raw value; after cleanup, this line rethrows that error unchanged to the caller. This leaks the credential through ordinary error logging despite authenticated conformance's redaction contract. Redact setup/close failures while the immutable credential snapshot is still available before storing or rethrowing them.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6iD65W\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/testing/src/suite.ts\",\n      \"line\": 226,\n      \"originalLine\": 136,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-14T09:52:32Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r4004012235\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Scrub scenario errors before their size truncation**\\n\\nFor an authenticated target whose server reflects the credential near the `scenarioRun.maxErrorBytes` boundary, `runScenarios()` applies `truncateError()` before returning these results. That leaves only a credential prefix, so the final exact-value target scrub cannot recognize it and the JSON/JUnit report leaks the prefix. Fresh evidence after the target scrubber ordering fix is that scenario results arrive here already truncated; apply target-aware scrubbing before scenario error truncation.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6iG9eR\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/cli/src/index.ts\",\n      \"line\": 581,\n      \"originalLine\": 438,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-14T12:39:03Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r4005212061\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Reject forbidden API-key headers during CLI parsing**\\n\\nWhen `inspect` or `test-target` receives a syntactically valid but prohibited header such as `--api-key-header host` or `content-length`, this check accepts the configuration and defers rejection to target opening. `test-target` then emits a failed runtime report and exit 1 (and `inspect` likewise classifies the client failure as runtime), although the CLI documents malformed configuration as exit 2; validate the header against the credential-header policy here before constructing the target.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6iXUYB\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/testing/src/host-authorization.ts\",\n      \"line\": 261,\n      \"originalLine\": 260,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-15T03:47:11Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r4011736872\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Honor the configured authorization-code fixture**\\n\\nWhen a caller sets `fixture.token.code` to exercise an invalid, expired, or independently authored authorization code, the runner ignores it and always submits the code returned by the authorization redirect. Such a negative token-exchange fixture can therefore pass while never testing its configured input; use the token-request fixture's `code` when supplied, or remove the public field so it cannot silently misconfigure an experiment.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6ify-K\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/inspector/tests/authenticated-artifacts.test.ts\",\n      \"line\": 246,\n      \"originalLine\": 220,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-15T11:33:34Z\",\n      \"author\": \"cubic-dev-ai\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r4015100997\",\n      \"body\": \"\u003c!-- cubic:v=0645f387-8083-48da-aee7-d3979d8e8672 --\u003e\\n\u003c!-- cubic:review-run=ef4f688d-c951-5e37-a081-804532482b41 --\u003e\\n\u003c!-- metadata:{\\\"confidence\\\":6} --\u003e\\nP2: The custom redactor in this test substitutes only substrings equal to \\\"opaque-property-key\\\" in the serialized JSON, so a stored value of \\\"value\\\" (which contains no secret) is left untouched. After exportScenario redacts the operation arguments and structuredContent, the result is { \\\"${MCPFN_SECRET}\\\": \\\"value\\\" }, not { \\\"${MCPFN_SECRET}\\\": \\\"${MCPFN_SECRET}\\\" }. The two toEqual assertions on the value therefore fail, so this test breaks the @mcpfn/inspector suite (or, if the suite is green, is not exercising the behavior it claims). The point of the test is key redaction, so assert the unchanged value.\\n\\n\u003cdetails\u003e\\n\u003csummary\u003ePrompt for AI agents\u003c/summary\u003e\\n\\n```text\\nCheck if this issue is valid — if so, understand the root cause and fix it. At mcpfn/inspector/tests/authenticated-artifacts.test.ts, line 220:\\n\\n\u003ccomment\u003eThe custom redactor in this test substitutes only substrings equal to \\\"opaque-property-key\\\" in the serialized JSON, so a stored value of \\\"value\\\" (which contains no secret) is left untouched. After exportScenario redacts the operation arguments and structuredContent, the result is { \\\"${MCPFN_SECRET}\\\": \\\"value\\\" }, not { \\\"${MCPFN_SECRET}\\\": \\\"${MCPFN_SECRET}\\\" }. The two toEqual assertions on the value therefore fail, so this test breaks the @mcpfn/inspector suite (or, if the suite is green, is not exercising the behavior it claims). The point of the test is key redaction, so assert the unchanged value.\u003c/comment\u003e\\n\\n\u003cfile context\u003e\\n@@ -140,6 +201,28 @@ it.each([\\\"tools.call\\\", \\\"non-idempotent\\\", \\\"none\\\"])(\\\"preserves exported scenario d\\n+    { content: [], structuredContent: { [secret]: \\\"value\\\" } },\\n+  );\\n+  expect(scenario.variables).toEqual([\\\"MCPFN_SECRET\\\"]);\\n+  expect(scenario.arguments).toEqual({ \\\"${MCPFN_SECRET}\\\": \\\"${MCPFN_SECRET}\\\" });\\n+  expect(scenario.expect?.structuredContent).toEqual({\\n+    \\\"${MCPFN_SECRET}\\\": \\\"${MCPFN_SECRET}\\\",\\n\u003c/file context\u003e\\n```\\n\\n\u003c/details\u003e\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6i7PnT\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/testing/src/host-authorization.ts\",\n      \"line\": 449,\n      \"originalLine\": 445,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-16T12:25:35Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r4025972715\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Require no-store headers on successful token responses**\\n\\nWhen a hosted authorization server returns a valid-looking 200 JSON token set but omits `Cache-Control: no-store` or `Pragma: no-cache`, this validator accepts both the exchange and refresh response. RFC 6749 §5.1 requires those headers for responses containing tokens, so the hosted regression can report an insecure, nonconforming provider as passed; validate both cache directives before accepting the token set.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jtJst\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/cli/src/index.ts\",\n      \"line\": 520,\n      \"originalLine\": 434,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-18T10:25:30Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r4045892488\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Mark truncated conformance reports incomplete**\\n\\nWhen `--max-report-bytes` is smaller than an otherwise successful conformance result, this replaces the captured streams with `[TRUNCATED]` but leaves `ok: true` and `exitCode: 0`, and the CLI consequently exits successfully. The resulting machine artifact silently omits evidence without any truncation or incompleteness field; record the truncation in the report and make the artifact non-passing, as the other bounded report paths do.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jtKNB\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/cli/src/bin.ts\",\n      \"line\": 31,\n      \"originalLine\": 26,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-18T10:25:59Z\",\n      \"author\": \"cubic-dev-ai\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r4045895485\",\n      \"body\": \"\u003c!-- cubic:v=1751549d-12ec-4215-87aa-bdb7a88c835b --\u003e\\n\u003c!-- cubic:review-run=a8ca27b3-ecd1-5754-a86a-a9042c91c442 --\u003e\\n\u003c!-- metadata:{\\\"confidence\\\":9} --\u003e\\nP2: When a target or conformance command emits a report and cleanup retry fails, `runCli` has already queued non-awaited stdout output. This forced exit can terminate before buffered report bytes reach CI or a pipe; flush stdout before exiting, or make CLI output writes awaitable.\\n\\n\u003cdetails\u003e\\n\u003csummary\u003ePrompt for AI agents\u003c/summary\u003e\\n\\n```text\\nCheck if this issue is valid — if so, understand the root cause and fix it. At mcpfn/cli/src/bin.ts, line 26:\\n\\n\u003ccomment\u003eWhen a target or conformance command emits a report and cleanup retry fails, `runCli` has already queued non-awaited stdout output. This forced exit can terminate before buffered report bytes reach CI or a pipe; flush stdout before exiting, or make CLI output writes awaitable.\u003c/comment\u003e\\n\\n\u003cfile context\u003e\\n@@ -1,12 +1,32 @@\\n+    ) {\\n+      const message = error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error);\\n+      process.stderr.write(`${message}\\\\n`, () =\u003e {\\n+        process.exit(MCPFN_CLI_EXIT_TEST_FAILURE);\\n+      });\\n+      return;\\n\u003c/file context\u003e\\n```\\n\\n\u003c/details\u003e\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jvE9Q\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/testing/src/remote-target.ts\",\n      \"line\": 531,\n      \"originalLine\": 495,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-18T12:19:10Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r4046644273\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Report OAuth revocation failures in the correct phase**\\n\\nWhen transport shutdown succeeds but an OAuth credential provider's `revoke()` rejects, this hard-coded event reports `transport-close` even though the lease identifies the failure as `token-revocation`. Diagnostic consumers and suite timelines consequently attribute an authorization-server cleanup failure to transport/preflight cleanup; distinguish handle-close failures from release failures and use `lease.cleanupPhase()` after the transport has closed.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jvkZ0\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/testing/src/remote-target.ts\",\n      \"line\": 37,\n      \"originalLine\": 37,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-18T12:46:14Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r4046835594\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Parse structured cookie credentials before redaction**\\n\\nWhen a custom credential uses a structured header such as `Cookie: session=opaque-secret`, only the entire header value is registered as secret; a server response that reflects the cookie value alone (for example, `{ \\\"user\\\": \\\"opaque-secret\\\" }`) therefore passes through client events, JSON/JUnit reports, and inspector artifacts unchanged. Parse recognized structured credential headers such as `Cookie`, or reject them when their credential components cannot be registered safely.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jvkZ9\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/cli/src/index.ts\",\n      \"line\": 414,\n      \"originalLine\": 406,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-18T12:46:14Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r4046835604\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Classify report-write failures as runtime failures**\\n\\nWhen a `test-target` run succeeds but writing `--output` fails (for example, due to permissions or a full filesystem), this plain filesystem error reaches the outer handler and is classified as usage exit code 2 because it is not one of the recognized runtime error classes. This occurs after argument parsing and target execution, and the analogous `inspect` path explicitly wraps output failures as runtime failures, so this path should likewise return the documented test/runtime exit code 1.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jwTTI\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/cli/src/index.ts\",\n      \"line\": 293,\n      \"originalLine\": 293,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-18T13:25:27Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r4047120637\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Classify conformance output failures as test failures**\\n\\nWhen `conformance --report` cannot write its file, or an injected stdout/stderr sink rejects, this call supplies no `failureMessage`, so `preserveCleanupOwner()` rethrows the raw I/O error and the outer handler returns usage exit code 2. These failures occur after valid argument parsing and conformance execution, while the documented code 2 is reserved for invalid configuration/source/usage; wrap this path as an `McpFnClientError` so operational report-output failures return code 1.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jwtNJ\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/testing/src/suite.ts\",\n      \"line\": 379,\n      \"originalLine\": 379,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-18T13:46:02Z\",\n      \"author\": \"cubic-dev-ai\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r4047279319\",\n      \"body\": \"\u003c!-- cubic:v=83ff363b-c542-461e-be41-44ff7bf43d44 --\u003e\\n\u003c!-- cubic:review-run=8d29ee6d-ae5b-5852-ba85-dd7a7d4dc128 --\u003e\\n\u003c!-- metadata:{\\\"confidence\\\":9} --\u003e\\nP3: When target descriptor projection fails for a non-credential reason, this branch labels the report as a credential-redaction failure. Track redaction failure separately from descriptor/safe-serialization projection failure before selecting this reason.\\n\\n\u003cdetails\u003e\\n\u003csummary\u003ePrompt for AI agents\u003c/summary\u003e\\n\\n```text\\nCheck if this issue is valid — if so, understand the root cause and fix it. At mcpfn/testing/src/suite.ts, line 379:\\n\\n\u003ccomment\u003eWhen target descriptor projection fails for a non-credential reason, this branch labels the report as a credential-redaction failure. Track redaction failure separately from descriptor/safe-serialization projection failure before selecting this reason.\u003c/comment\u003e\\n\\n\u003cfile context\u003e\\n@@ -372,7 +375,9 @@ async function runTargetSuite(options: RunMcpFnTargetSuiteOptions): Promise\u003cMcpF\\n         ? \\\"Credential redaction exceeded its traversal budget\\\"\\n-        : \\\"Report content omitted because safe serialization failed\\\",\\n+        : projectionFailed\\n+          ? \\\"Credential redaction failed; report content omitted because safe serialization failed\\\"\\n+          : \\\"Report content omitted because safe serialization failed\\\",\\n       target: { kind: \\\"custom\\\" }, server: undefined, capabilities: undefined, manifestHash: undefined,\\n\u003c/file context\u003e\\n```\\n\\n\u003c/details\u003e\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jwvKs\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/cli/src/index.ts\",\n      \"line\": 305,\n      \"originalLine\": 305,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-18T13:47:27Z\",\n      \"author\": \"pullfrog\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r4047291549\",\n      \"body\": \"**[P2] Keep this classification stable when stderr remains unavailable**\\n\\nThe wrapper converts the first stderr rejection into a safe runtime error, but the outer catch writes that diagnostic through the same sink without protection, so a persistently rejecting sink makes `runCli()` reject instead of returning exit code `1`. Make the diagnostic write best effort for this classified failure and extend the test so its stderr callback rejects every invocation.\\n\\n\u003cdetails\u003e\u003csummary\u003eTechnical details\u003c/summary\u003e\\n\\n````markdown\\n# Preserve runtime classification across persistent stderr failures\\n\\n## Affected sites\\n- `mcpfn/cli/src/index.ts:305` — classifies the conformance output rejection as `McpFnClientError`.\\n- `mcpfn/cli/src/index.ts:467` — retries the same stderr sink without a guard and can replace the classified result with another rejection.\\n- `mcpfn/cli/tests/conformance-cleanup.test.ts:205-212` — rejects only the first stderr call, allowing the second write to succeed.\\n\\n## Required outcome\\n- A conformance stderr sink that rejects on every call must not expose its rejection or change the stable runtime-failure disposition.\\n- Regression coverage must exercise a persistently failing sink rather than a one-shot failure.\\n````\\n\\n\u003c/details\u003e\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jwwv0\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/testing/src/remote-target.ts\",\n      \"line\": 266,\n      \"originalLine\": 266,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-18T13:48:39Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r4047301459\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P1 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P1-orange?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Reject credentials matching preserved schema keys**\\n\\nWhen an opaque credential equals or is contained in a required envelope key—for example, an API key of `timeline`—`preserveStructuralKey()` records the matching pattern, and this filter removes it from the final serialized-output check while the literal key remains in the report. Fresh evidence after the discriminator-value collision check is that schema key names themselves are still exempt, so target-suite and inspector JSON can expose the credential; reject these credential/key collisions rather than excluding their patterns.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"PRRT_kwDOP-e7KM6jwwv5\",\n      \"path\": \"mcpfn/client/src/client.ts\",\n      \"line\": 1035,\n      \"originalLine\": 1035,\n      \"comments\": 1,\n      \"lastCreatedAt\": \"2026-09-18T13:48:39Z\",\n      \"author\": \"chatgpt-codex-connector\",\n      \"url\": \"https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/pull/170#discussion_r4047301468\",\n      \"body\": \"**\u003csub\u003e\u003csub\u003e![P2 Badge](https://img.shields.io/badge/P2-yellow?style=flat)\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/sub\u003e  Preserve or omit diagnostic target discriminators**\\n\\nWhen a target credential equals `kind` or overlaps the target's `kind` value, payload-mode redaction rewrites that required property name or discriminator, but the diagnostic is still dispatched without incrementing the omission count. Fresh evidence after suite-level target-kind reconstruction is that live diagnostics still take this path, so inspector and suite timelines can report `timelineComplete: true` while containing a malformed or misidentified `event.target`; validate and reconstruct the target kind or mark the diagnostic as omitted.\\n\\nUseful? React with 👍 / 👎.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}","type":"input_text"}],"type":"custom_tool_call_output"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aad4eed26c087d090ce60a445e5adb2","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"summary":[{"text":"**Assessing new unresolved threads**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Redesigning error classification and handling**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Inspecting structural key credential handling**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aad4ef6f0dc87d09341f50d07578d4c","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"summary":[{"text":"**Planning shared helper for target descriptor preservation**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Designing robust target descriptor redaction helper**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Verifying fallback target validation helpers**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"encrypted_content":"REDACTED","id":"rs_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aad4f00334487d0adbeafd7d034815c","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"summary":[{"text":"**Categorizing root cause families**","type":"summary_text"},{"text":"**Planning thorough code inspection**","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning"},{"content":[{"text":"The complete usable snapshot is now established at stable head `dfaeef0d1…`: 325 threads, 90 unresolved (39 current-hunk, 51 outdated-only), 220 reviews, 64 issue comments, 364 inline comments, and all 18 checks terminal and successful/neutral/skipped. Four new current findings form four root causes across three recurring contracts: structural credential safety, report failure taxonomy, and runtime-output classification. I’m tracing the shared producers and consumers now, then I’ll record the mandatory round-9 reassessment before any code edit.","type":"output_text"}],"id":"msg_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aad4f03734887d09e79bee8942361d0","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"content_item_kinds":["unknown"],"create_time":1789742827.98141,"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"phase":"commentary","role":"assistant","type":"message"},{"call_id":"call_z22gWSnUFqeqzqTzh456auiG","id":"ctc_05349b17f5ad9d6a016aad4f05676887d09da30a4467aca3cc","input":"const r = await tools.exec_command({\n  cmd: \"sed -n '130,300p' mcpfn/testing/src/remote-target.ts\\nsed -n '220,390p' mcpfn/testing/src/suite.ts\\nsed -n '950,1095p' mcpfn/client/src/client.ts\\nsed -n '440,475p' mcpfn/cli/src/index.ts\\nrg -n \\\"structural key|preserveStructuralKey|duplicate object keys|target.*kind|redaction omission|safe serialization failed|stderr.*reject|runtime exit 1\\\" mcpfn/client/tests mcpfn/testing/tests mcpfn/cli/tests --glob '*.ts'\",\n  workdir: \"/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/worktrees/superfunctions/PR-170\",\n  yield_time_ms: 10000,\n  max_output_tokens: 40000\n});\ntext(r.output);","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789742827.98141,"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"name":"exec","status":"completed","type":"custom_tool_call"},{"call_id":"call_z22gWSnUFqeqzqTzh456auiG","id":"ctco_01a0b4fc-bcbb-7501-8f44-59202c09bfe4","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"create_time":1789742857.403688,"turn_id":"01a0b4f9-6008-7481-8c13-f3c8d9cbf433"},"output":[{"text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.5 seconds\nOutput:\n","type":"input_text"},{"text":"  }\n  return input;\n}\n\nfunction scrubCredentials\u003cT\u003e(value: T, values: Iterable\u003cstring\u003e, preserveKeys = false, redactionMarker?: string): T {\n  // Preserve whether a pattern came from opaque text or a serializer. Literal\n  // percent sequences in credentials must not acquire URL hex-case semantics.\n  const variants = [...values].filter(Boolean).flatMap(secret =\u003e [\n    { value: secret, encoded: false },\n    { value: JSON.stringify(secret).slice(1, -1), encoded: false },\n    { value: encodeURIComponent(secret), encoded: true },\n    { value: new URLSearchParams({ value: secret }).toString().slice(\"value=\".length), encoded: true },\n  ]).sort((a, b) =\u003e b.value.length - a.value.length);\n  let entries = 0, stringBytes = 0;\n  const budget = (input: unknown, depth = 0): void =\u003e {\n    input = specialValue(input);\n    if ((Array.isArray(input) \u0026\u0026 input.length \u003e 100_000) || ++entries \u003e 100_000 || depth \u003e 32) throw new McpFnRedactionLimitError();\n    if (typeof input === \"string\") {\n      stringBytes += Buffer.byteLength(input);\n      if (input.length \u003e 262_144 || stringBytes \u003e 2_097_152) throw new McpFnRedactionLimitError();\n    } else if (input \u0026\u0026 typeof input === \"object\") {\n      for (const key in input) if (Object.hasOwn(input, key)) {\n        budget(key, depth + 1);\n        budget((input as Record\u003cstring, unknown\u003e)[key], depth + 1);\n      }\n    }\n  };\n  // Check before either redactor allocates copies. Exceeding a budget is an\n  // explicit failure, never silent truncation of a typed report collection.\n  budget(value);\n  const patterns = variants.map(({ value, encoded }) =\u003e {\n    const literal = value.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\\]\\\\]/g, \"\\\\$\u0026\");\n    // Only generated percent escapes accept mixed-case hex digits.\n    return encoded\n      ? literal.replace(/%[0-9A-Fa-f]{2}/g, escape =\u003e escape.replace(/[A-Fa-f]/g, hex =\u003e `[${hex.toLowerCase()}${hex.toUpperCase()}]`))\n      : literal;\n  });\n  const secretPattern = patterns.length ? new RegExp(patterns.join(\"|\"), \"g\") : undefined;\n  const replacementPattern = patterns.length ? new RegExp(patterns.join(\"|\")) : undefined;\n  const structuralKeyMatchers = patterns.map((pattern) =\u003e ({\n    pattern,\n    expression: new RegExp(pattern),\n  }));\n  const structuralKeyPatterns = new Set\u003cstring\u003e();\n  const preserveStructuralKey = (input: string): string =\u003e {\n    for (const { pattern, expression } of structuralKeyMatchers) {\n      if (expression.test(input)) structuralKeyPatterns.add(pattern);\n    }\n    return input;\n  };\n  const preserveStructural = (input: string): string =\u003e {\n    if (replacementPattern?.test(input)) {\n      throw new McpFnRedactionLimitError(\n        \"Credential collides with a required structural artifact field\",\n      );\n    }\n    return input;\n  };\n  // A requested marker is itself output and must not reproduce a credential.\n  if (secretPattern) {\n    const markerContainsSecret = secretPattern.test(redactionMarker ?? \"[REDACTED]\");\n    secretPattern.lastIndex = 0;\n    if (markerContainsSecret) redactionMarker = \"\";\n  }\n  const clientKinds = new Set([\"logging.message\", \"progress\", \"tasks.status\", \"resources.updated\", \"tools.list_changed\", \"resources.list_changed\", \"prompts.list_changed\", \"resources.subscribed\", \"resources.unsubscribed\", \"client.roots\", \"client.sampling\", \"client.elicitation\"]);\n  const scrub = (input: unknown, role = \"payload\", field = \"\", finalPass = false): unknown =\u003e {\n    input = specialValue(input);\n    if (typeof input === \"string\") {\n      if ((role === \"root\" || role === \"inspectorEvent\") \u0026\u0026 field === \"kind\" \u0026\u0026 clientKinds.has(input)) return preserveStructural(input);\n      if (role === \"root\" \u0026\u0026 field === \"clientState\" \u0026\u0026 [\"idle\", \"connecting\", \"authorization-required\", \"connected\", \"closing\", \"closed\"].includes(input)) return preserveStructural(input);\n      if (role === \"target\" \u0026\u0026 field === \"kind\" \u0026\u0026 [\"authenticated-streamable-http\", \"streamable-http\", \"stdio\", \"in-memory\", \"custom\"].includes(input)) return preserveStructural(input);\n      if ((role === \"packages\" \u0026\u0026 field === \"testing\") || (role === \"runtime\" \u0026\u0026 [\"node\", \"reportSchemaVersion\"].includes(field)) || (role === \"root\" \u0026\u0026 field === \"suiteVersion\")) return preserveStructural(input);\n      if ((role === \"root\" || role === \"result\") \u0026\u0026 field === \"status\" \u0026\u0026 [\"passed\", \"failed\", \"incomplete\", \"complete\"].includes(input)) return preserveStructural(input);\n      if ((role === \"root\" || role === \"diagnostic\") \u0026\u0026 field === \"outcome\" \u0026\u0026 [\"started\", \"succeeded\", \"failed\"].includes(input)) return preserveStructural(input);\n      if (role === \"root\" \u0026\u0026 field === \"kind\" \u0026\u0026 [\"mcpfn.target-suite-report\", \"mcpfn.inspector-snapshot\", \"mcpfn.official-conformance-report\"].includes(input)) return preserveStructural(input);\n      if ((role === \"root\" || role === \"diagnostic\" || role === \"failure\") \u0026\u0026 field === \"phase\" \u0026\u0026 [\"resource-discovery\", \"authorization-server-discovery\", \"client-registration\", \"authorization-request\", \"authorization-callback\", \"token-exchange\", \"token-refresh\", \"token-revocation\", \"transport-connect\", \"mcp-initialize\", \"capability-operation\", \"transport-close\"].includes(input)) return preserveStructural(input);\n      if (role === \"failure\" \u0026\u0026 field === \"layer\" \u0026\u0026 [\"mcpfn-preflight\", \"authorization-server\", \"resource-server\", \"mcp-initialization\", \"scenario\", \"upstream-conformance\"].includes(input)) return preserveStructural(input);\n      if (role === \"inspectorEvent\" \u0026\u0026 field === \"source\" \u0026\u0026 [\"diagnostic\", \"client\"].includes(input)) return preserveStructural(input);\n      if (role === \"result\" \u0026\u0026 field === \"sideEffect\" \u0026\u0026 [\"none\", \"idempotent\", \"non-idempotent\"].includes(input)) return preserveStructural(input);\n      // Whole-string rejection cannot compose another credential by joining pieces.\n      // Run after both redactors; never feed this result through another redactor.\n      if (finalPass) return replacementPattern?.test(input) ? \"\" : input;\n      return secretPattern ? input.replace(secretPattern, secret =\u003e {\n        const replacement = redactionMarker ?? (secret.length \u003c 10 ? (secret.includes(\"*\") ? \"#\" : \"*\").repeat(secret.length) : \"[REDACTED]\");\n        // Every generated mask must be safe against the entire credential set.\n        return replacementPattern!.test(replacement) ? \"\" : replacement;\n      }) : input;\n    }\n    if (Array.isArray(input)) {\n      const result = input.map(entry =\u003e scrub(entry, role, \"\", finalPass));\n      assertPayloadSerialization(result, role, finalPass, replacementPattern);\n      return result;\n    }\n    if (input \u0026\u0026 typeof input === \"object\") {\n      const entries: Array\u003c[string, unknown]\u003e = [];\n      const keys = new Set\u003cstring\u003e();\n      for (const [key, entry] of Object.entries(input)) {\n        const fixed = envelopeKeys[role]?.has(key) ?? false;\n        if (fixed) preserveStructuralKey(key);\n        let childRole = \"payload\";\n        if (fixed) {\n          if (key === \"results\") childRole = \"result\";\n          else if (key === \"timeline\") childRole = (value as any)?.kind === \"mcpfn.inspector-snapshot\" ? \"inspectorEvent\" : \"diagnostic\";\n          else if ([\"failure\", \"runtime\", \"packages\", \"droppedInventoryEntries\", \"target\"].includes(key)) childRole = key;\n          else if (role === \"inspectorEvent\" \u0026\u0026 key === \"event\") childRole = (input as { source?: string }).source === \"client\" ? \"root\" : \"diagnostic\";\n        }\n        const scrubbedKey = fixed ? key : scrub(key, \"payload\", \"\", finalPass) as string;\n        if (keys.has(scrubbedKey)) {\n          throw new McpFnRedactionLimitError(\n            \"Credential redaction created duplicate object keys\",\n          );\n        }\n        keys.add(scrubbedKey);\n        entries.push([\n          scrubbedKey,\n          typeof entry === \"string\"\n            ? scrub(entry, fixed ? role : \"payload\", key, finalPass)\n            : scrub(entry, childRole, \"\", finalPass),\n        ]);\n      }\n      const result = Object.fromEntries(entries);\n      assertPayloadSerialization(result, role, finalPass, replacementPattern);\n      return result;\n    }\n    return input;\n  };\n  const timelineEvent = value \u0026\u0026 typeof value === \"object\" \u0026\u0026 [\"client\", \"diagnostic\"].includes((value as { source?: string }).source ?? \"\");\n  const role = preserveKeys ? (timelineEvent ? \"inspectorEvent\" : \"root\") : \"payload\";\n  const scrubbed = scrub(value, role);\n  const generic = redactOAuthValue(scrubbed, { maxStringLength: 262_144, maxDepth: 64, maxArrayEntries: 100_000, maxObjectEntries: 100_000, ...(redactionMarker !== undefined ? { redactionMarker } : {}) });\n  const result = scrub(generic, role, \"\", true) as T;\n  const boundaryPatterns = patterns.filter((pattern) =\u003e !structuralKeyPatterns.has(pattern));\n  assertPayloadSerialization(\n    result,\n    \"payload\",\n    true,\n    boundaryPatterns.length ? new RegExp(boundaryPatterns.join(\"|\")) : undefined,\n  );\n  return result;\n}\n\nfunction assertPayloadSerialization(\n  value: unknown,\n  role: string,\n  finalPass: boolean,\n  credentialPattern: RegExp | undefined,\n): void {\n  if (!finalPass || role !== \"payload\" || !credentialPattern) return;\n  const serialized = JSON.stringify(value);\n  if (serialized !== undefined \u0026\u0026 credentialPattern.test(serialized)) {\n    throw new McpFnRedactionLimitError(\n      \"Credential redaction could not guarantee safe serialized output\",\n    );\n  }\n}\n\n/** Remove known opaque credential values as well as credential-shaped fields. */\nexport function redactTargetCredentials\u003cT\u003e(target: McpFnTarget, value: T, options: { preserveKeys?: boolean; redactionMarker?: string } = {}): T {\n  const state = targetSecrets.get(target);\n  return scrubCredentials(value, new Set([...(state?.active.keys() ?? []), ...[...(state?.scopes ?? [])].flatMap((scope) =\u003e [...scope])]), options.preserveKeys, options.redactionMarker);\n}\n\n/** Redact authenticated conformance output using the acquired credential. */\nexport function redactRemoteCredential\u003cT\u003e(credential: McpFnRemoteCredential, value: T, options: { preserveKeys?: boolean } = {}): T {\n  return scrubCredentials(value, credentialValues(credential.headers), options.preserveKeys);\n}\n\nconst MAX_CREDENTIAL_HEADERS = 32;\nconst MAX_CREDENTIAL_HEADER_BYTES = 16_384;\nconst MAX_CREDENTIAL_HEADER_VALUE_BYTES = 8_192;\nconst FORBIDDEN_CREDENTIAL_HEADERS = new Set([\n    }\n    execution = {\n      results: await runScenarios(\n        client,\n        options.scenarios ?? [],\n        options.scenarioRun,\n      ),\n      server: client.session.getServerVersion(),\n      capabilities: client.session.getServerCapabilities(),\n    };\n  } catch (error) {\n    try {\n      failure = normalizeMcpFnReportFailure(redactSuiteArtifact(options.target, error, { preserveKeys: true }));\n    } catch {\n      // Arbitrary error properties/proxies can throw, including secret-bearing errors.\n      failure = normalizeMcpFnReportFailure({ name: \"RedactionError\", code: \"MCPFN_REDACTION_FAILED\", message: \"Target failure omitted because credential redaction failed\" });\n    }\n  } finally {\n    try {\n      // Custom hooks may depend on credentials cleared by close. Capture every\n      // target-controlled report value while the session still owns that state.\n      if (client?.session.state === \"connected\") {\n        const { kind, ...descriptor } = options.target.describe();\n        capturedTarget = {\n          ...redactSuiteArtifact(options.target, descriptor, { preserveKeys: false }),\n          kind: client.session.preserveArtifactStructure(kind),\n        };\n        capturedManifestHash = options.manifest\n          ? redactSuiteArtifact(options.target, options.manifest.hash, { preserveKeys: false })\n          : undefined;\n        execution = {\n          server: execution.server === undefined\n            ? undefined\n            : redactSuiteArtifact(options.target, execution.server, { preserveKeys: false }),\n          capabilities: execution.capabilities === undefined\n            ? undefined\n            : redactSuiteArtifact(options.target, execution.capabilities, { preserveKeys: false }),\n          results: execution.results.map(result =\u003e ({\n            ...result,\n            name: redactSuiteArtifact(options.target, result.name, { preserveKeys: false }),\n            operation: redactSuiteArtifact(options.target, result.operation, { preserveKeys: false }),\n            ...(result.tool === undefined ? {} : {\n              tool: redactSuiteArtifact(options.target, result.tool, { preserveKeys: false }),\n            }),\n          })),\n        };\n      } else if (execution.results.length || execution.server || execution.capabilities) {\n        projectionFailed = true;\n      }\n    } catch { projectionFailed = true; }\n    try {\n      await client?.close();\n    } catch (error) {\n      const owner = client;\n      if (error instanceof McpFnTestClientCleanupError) {\n        retainedCleanup = () =\u003e error.retryCleanup();\n      } else if (owner) {\n        retainedCleanup = () =\u003e owner.close();\n      }\n      const terminalCleanupError = error instanceof McpFnTestClientCleanupError \u0026\u0026 error.cause !== undefined\n        ? error.cause\n        : error;\n      const terminalCleanupPhase = terminalCleanupError \u0026\u0026 typeof terminalCleanupError === \"object\" \u0026\u0026\n          (terminalCleanupError as { phase?: unknown }).phase === \"token-revocation\"\n        ? \"token-revocation\"\n        : \"transport-close\";\n      cleanupFailure = normalizeMcpFnReportFailure({\n        name: \"CleanupError\",\n        message: \"Target cleanup failed\",\n        code: \"MCPFN_TARGET_CLEANUP_FAILED\",\n        phase: terminalCleanupPhase,\n      });\n      if (!failure) failure = cleanupFailure;\n    }\n    const diagnosticRedactionOmissions = client?.session\n      .getRedactionOmissionCounts().diagnostics ?? initialDiagnosticRedactionOmissions;\n    const unobservedDiagnosticOmissions = Math.max(\n      0,\n      diagnosticRedactionOmissions - initialDiagnosticRedactionOmissions -\n        observedDiagnosticRedactionOmissions,\n    );\n    if (unobservedDiagnosticOmissions \u003e 0) {\n      timelineRedactionFailed = true;\n      droppedTimelineEvents += unobservedDiagnosticOmissions;\n    }\n  }\n  failure ??= cleanupFailure;\n  const results = execution.results;\n  const failed = results.filter((result) =\u003e result.status === \"failed\").length;\n  const incomplete = results.filter((result) =\u003e result.status === \"incomplete\").length;\n  const droppedObservedEvents = results.reduce(\n    (total, result) =\u003e total + (result.droppedObservedEvents ?? 0),\n    0,\n  );\n  const redactionOmittedObservedEvents = results.reduce(\n    (total, result) =\u003e total + (result.redactionOmittedObservedEvents ?? 0),\n    0,\n  );\n  const overflowedObservedEvents = droppedObservedEvents - redactionOmittedObservedEvents;\n  const artifactIncomplete = Boolean(failure) || incomplete \u003e 0 ||\n    droppedTimelineEvents \u003e 0 ||\n    droppedObservedEvents \u003e 0;\n  const report: McpFnTargetSuiteReport = {\n    formatVersion: 1,\n    kind: \"mcpfn.target-suite-report\",\n    status: artifactIncomplete ? \"incomplete\" : \"complete\",\n    runtime: {\n      node: process.version,\n      scenarioFormatVersion: 1,\n      reportSchemaVersion: MCPFN_REPORT_SCHEMA_VERSION,\n      packages: { testing: MCPFN_TESTING_VERSION },\n    },\n    ok: failed === 0 \u0026\u0026 !artifactIncomplete,\n    target: capturedTarget,\n    server: execution.server,\n    capabilities: execution.capabilities,\n    manifestChecked,\n    ...(capturedManifestHash === undefined ? {} : { manifestHash: capturedManifestHash }),\n    total: results.length,\n    passed: results.length - failed - incomplete,\n    failed,\n    incomplete,\n    droppedResults: 0,\n    droppedObservedEvents,\n    ...(redactionOmittedObservedEvents \u003e 0 ? { redactionOmittedObservedEvents } : {}),\n    ...(failure || droppedTimelineEvents \u003e 0 || droppedObservedEvents \u003e 0\n      ? {\n        incompleteReason: [\n          ...(failure ? [`${failure.layer}: ${failure.message}`] : []),\n          ...(cleanupFailure ? [`Cleanup: ${cleanupFailure.message}`] : []),\n          ...(timelineSerializationFailed ? [\"Diagnostic timeline contained non-JSON data\"] : []),\n          ...(timelineRedactionFailed ? [\"Diagnostic timeline redaction failed; original events omitted\"] : []),\n          ...(timelineCountExceeded ? [\"Diagnostic timeline exceeded maxTimelineEvents\"] : []),\n          ...(timelineBytesExceeded ? [\"Diagnostic timeline exceeded maxReportBytes\"] : []),\n          ...(redactionOmittedObservedEvents \u003e 0\n            ? [\"Observed client events were omitted because credential redaction failed\"]\n            : []),\n          ...(overflowedObservedEvents \u003e 0\n            ? [\"Observed client events exceeded maxObservedEvents\"]\n            : []),\n        ].join(\"; \"),\n      }\n      : {}),\n    ...(failure ? { failure } : {}),\n    timeline,\n    droppedTimelineEvents,\n    results,\n  };\n  let finalized: McpFnTargetSuiteReport;\n  try {\n    if (projectionFailed) throw new Error(\"Report payload redaction failed\");\n    // Custom redaction already completed before cleanup. Built-in report scopes\n    // remain active here for bounded final serialization.\n    finalized = enforceReportCap(redactTargetCredentials(options.target, report, { preserveKeys: true }), maxReportBytes);\n  } catch (error) {\n    finalized = enforceReportCap({ ...report, ok: false, status: \"incomplete\",\n      incompleteReason: error instanceof McpFnRedactionLimitError\n        ? \"Credential redaction exceeded its traversal budget\"\n        : projectionFailed\n          ? \"Credential redaction failed; report content omitted because safe serialization failed\"\n          : \"Report content omitted because safe serialization failed\",\n      target: { kind: \"custom\" }, server: undefined, capabilities: undefined, manifestHash: undefined,\n      failure: undefined, results: [], timeline: [],\n      droppedResults: report.results.length, droppedTimelineEvents: report.droppedTimelineEvents + report.timeline.length,\n    }, maxReportBytes);\n  }\n  if (retainedCleanup) throw new McpFnTargetSuiteCleanupError(finalized, retainedCleanup);\n  return finalized;\n}\n\nfunction enforceReportCap(\n  }\n\n  private observeProgress(options: RequestOptions | undefined, operation: string): RequestOptions {\n    return {\n      ...options,\n      onprogress: (progress) =\u003e {\n        void this.emitEvent(\"progress\", { operation, ...progress });\n        options?.onprogress?.(progress);\n      },\n    };\n  }\n\n  private async emitEvent(kind: McpFnClientEventKind, payload?: unknown): Promise\u003cvoid\u003e {\n    let event: McpFnClientEvent | undefined;\n    try {\n      const { kind: targetKind, ...descriptor } = this.options.target.describe();\n      const at = (this.options.clock?.() ?? new Date()).toISOString();\n      event = {\n        formatVersion: 1,\n        kind: this.preserveArtifactStructure(kind),\n        at: this.preserveArtifactTimestamp(at),\n        requestId: this.redact(this.requestId(), { preserveKeys: false }),\n        target: this.redact({ ...descriptor, kind: targetKind }, { preserveKeys: false }) as McpFnClientEvent[\"target\"],\n        ...(payload !== undefined\n          ? { payload: this.redact(payload, { preserveKeys: false }) }\n          : {}),\n      };\n    }\n    catch {\n      event = this.clientEventRedactionFailure();\n    }\n    if (!event) return;\n    await Promise.allSettled(\n      [...this.eventListeners].map(async (listener) =\u003e listener(event)),\n    );\n  }\n\n  private requestId(): string {\n    return this.options.requestId?.() ?? randomUUID();\n  }\n\n  private async emit(\n    phase: McpFnDiagnosticPhase,\n    outcome: McpFnDiagnosticEvent[\"outcome\"],\n    requestId: string,\n    code?: string,\n    details?: Record\u003cstring, unknown\u003e,\n  ): Promise\u003cvoid\u003e {\n    let event: McpFnDiagnosticEvent | undefined;\n    try {\n      event = {\n        phase,\n        outcome,\n        ...(code ? { code } : {}),\n        requestId,\n        at: (this.options.clock?.() ?? new Date()).toISOString(),\n        // Cleanup retries may follow partial release of custom credential state.\n        target: phase === \"transport-close\" ? { kind: \"custom\" } : this.options.target.describe(),\n        ...(details ? { details } : {}),\n      };\n    } catch {\n      // Diagnostic construction can fail before dispatch redacts the value.\n      // Never reject a background protocol callback with an arbitrary error.\n      event = this.diagnosticRedactionFailure();\n    }\n    if (!event) return;\n    await this.dispatch(event);\n  }\n\n  private async dispatch(event: McpFnDiagnosticEvent): Promise\u003cvoid\u003e {\n    let redacted: McpFnDiagnosticEvent | undefined;\n    try {\n      if (this.isRedactionOmission(event)) {\n        redacted = event;\n      } else {\n        const { phase, outcome, code, requestId, at, target, details } = event;\n        const { kind, ...descriptor } = target;\n        redacted = {\n          phase: this.preserveArtifactStructure(phase),\n          outcome: this.preserveArtifactStructure(outcome),\n          ...(code === undefined\n            ? {}\n            : { code: this.redact(code, { preserveKeys: false }) }),\n          requestId: this.redact(requestId, { preserveKeys: false }),\n          at: this.preserveArtifactTimestamp(at),\n          target: this.redact({ ...descriptor, kind }, { preserveKeys: false }) as McpFnDiagnosticEvent[\"target\"],\n          ...(details === undefined\n            ? {}\n            : { details: this.redact(details, { preserveKeys: false }) }),\n        };\n      }\n    }\n    catch {\n      redacted = this.diagnosticRedactionFailure();\n    }\n    if (!redacted) return;\n    await Promise.allSettled(\n      [...this.listeners].map(async (listener) =\u003e listener(redacted)),\n    );\n  }\n\n  private safeArtifactStructure\u003cT extends string\u003e(values: readonly T[]): T | undefined {\n    for (const value of values) {\n      try { return this.preserveArtifactStructure(value); }\n      catch {}\n    }\n    return undefined;\n  }\n\n  private preserveArtifactTimestamp(value: string): string {\n    let canonical: string;\n    try { canonical = new Date(value).toISOString(); }\n    catch { canonical = \"\"; }\n    if (canonical !== value) {\n      throw new McpFnClientError(\n        \"MCPFN_OPERATION_FAILED\",\n        \"MCP artifact timestamp is not canonical ISO\",\n        { phase: \"capability-operation\" },\n      );\n    }\n    return this.preserveArtifactStructure(value);\n  }\n\n  private safeArtifactTimestamp(): string | undefined {\n    for (const value of [\n      new Date().toISOString(),\n      \"1970-01-01T00:00:00.000Z\",\n      \"2000-02-29T12:34:56.789Z\",\n    ]) {\n      try { return this.preserveArtifactTimestamp(value); }\n      catch {}\n    }\n    return undefined;\n  }\n\n  private clientEventRedactionFailure(): McpFnClientEvent | undefined {\n    this.clientEventRedactionOmissions += 1;\n    const kind = this.safeArtifactStructure(CLIENT_EVENT_KINDS);\n    const at = this.safeArtifactTimestamp();\n    if (!kind || !at) return undefined;\n    try {\n      const event: McpFnClientEvent = {\n        formatVersion: 1,\n        kind,\n        at,\n        requestId: this.redact(\"redacted\", { preserveKeys: false }),\n      if (options.output) {\n        await writeFile(path.resolve(cwd, options.output), serialized, \"utf8\");\n      }\n      await stdout(serialized);\n      if (!report.ok) exitCode = 1;\n    });\n\n  cli.help();\n  cli.version(MCPFN_CLI_VERSION);\n  try {\n    const parsed = cli.parse([\"node\", \"mcpfn\", ...argv], { run: false });\n    if (!cli.matchedCommand) {\n      if (parsed.options.help || parsed.options.version) return 0;\n      await stderr(argv.length ? `Unknown command: ${argv[0]}\\n` : \"A command is required\\n\");\n      return 2;\n    }\n    await cli.runMatchedCommand();\n  } catch (error) {\n    if (isCliCleanupError(error)) {\n      // Diagnostics are secondary to the live cleanup owner. A failed write\n      // must not prevent the bounded retry or replace the owning error.\n      try { await stderr(`${error.message}\\n`); } catch {}\n      // Preserve ownership when the bounded retry still fails. The executable\n      // entry point is the only layer allowed to terminate without a retry owner.\n      await error.retryCleanup();\n      return MCPFN_CLI_EXIT_TEST_FAILURE;\n    }\n    await stderr(`${error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)}\\n`);\n    if (error instanceof McpFnAssertionError || error instanceof McpFnClientError) {\n      return MCPFN_CLI_EXIT_TEST_FAILURE;\n    }\n    return MCPFN_CLI_EXIT_USAGE;\n  }\n  return exitCode;\n}\n\nmcpfn/cli/tests/cli.test.ts:364:  it(\"classifies target connection failures as runtime exit 1\", async () =\u003e {\nmcpfn/cli/tests/cli.test.ts:387:    \"classifies target-suite %s failures as safe runtime exit 1\",\nmcpfn/testing/tests/suite.test.ts:43:      target: { kind: \"fixture\", mode: \"external-shaped\" },\nmcpfn/testing/tests/suite.test.ts:59:      target: customTarget({ kind: \"never-opened\", open }),\nmcpfn/testing/tests/suite.test.ts:122:  it(\"distinguishes redaction omissions from observed-event overflow\", async () =\u003e {\nmcpfn/testing/tests/suite.test.ts:171:  const report = await runMcpFnTargetSuite({ target: customTarget({ kind: \"fixture\", 