own is `transport-close`; OAuth revocation is `token-revocation`; disposal after successful revocation returns to `transport-close`. A generic outer cleanup boundary must not overwrite a more specific trusted phase.\n- Producers / consumers: the authenticated HTTP target owns the credential lease and produces target diagnostics; `McpFnClient` coordinates handle/target cleanup and produces retryable client errors; `McpFnTestClient` retains the close owner; target-suite diagnostics, bounded reports, JUnit, CLI, and direct diagnostic consumers classify the resulting phase and failure layer.\n- Failure boundaries: raw transport close before credential release, OAuth `revoke()`, provider `dispose()`, client cleanup-drain wrapping, test-client error wrapping, and final suite report normalization.\n- Coordinated repair: throw a phase-bearing safe cleanup error from the credential lease; emit the lease phase only after transport shutdown succeeds; validate and preserve `token-revocation` through the client cleanup coordinator; and normalize the retained inner cleanup error in the target suite so a later generic close diagnostic cannot replace the terminal phase.\n- Verification criteria: direct target diagnostics must report transport failure as `transport-close`, OAuth revoke failure as `token-revocation`, and post-revocation disposal failure as `transport-close`; suite reports must map those phases to `authorization-server` and `mcpfn-preflight` respectively while retaining a retryable cleanup owner. Then client/testing focused tests and typechecks, the complete Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 7 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: credential cleanup errors may expose only a trusted phase, safe fixed text, and retry ownership; raw provider failures must not cross the lease, target, client, suite, inspector, or CLI boundary. Every independently reflectable credential component, including structured Cookie values, must be registered before artifacts are emitted. Target-suite post-execution artifact I/O failures are runtime failures and must not replace a retained cleanup owner.\n- Producers / consumers: credential providers produce header material and revoke/dispose failures; `credentialValues` and target secret scopes produce the redaction set; credential leases and authenticated targets own retryable cleanup; `McpFnClient` coordinates target/handle cleanup; suites, JUnit, inspector, and CLI serialize consumer-visible evidence. The target suite produces reports consumed by `runCli`, whose output writers and executable boundary classify failures.\n- Failure boundaries: structured Cookie parsing, pre-handle header validation/open failure, revoke/dispose rejection, target-level pending-release retries, client cleanup-drain/close wrapping, report/JUnit/stdout persistence, and final exit-code classification.\n- Coordinated repair: extract and validate Cookie credential components at the shared secret-discovery point; sanitize lease cleanup failures and client cleanup wrappers while preserving the trusted phase separately; rethrow the retained safe rejection from target-level cleanup instead of synthesizing `transport-close`; and convert target-suite artifact/output failures into a safe runtime error without displacing any cleanup owner.\n- Verification criteria: a reflected Cookie value must be absent from direct redaction and JSON/JUnit artifacts; a provider error containing a credential must not be reachable through cleanup cause chains; repeated OAuth revoke failure during a pre-handle open failure must remain `token-revocation` through client/suite reporting and later retry; target report/JUnit/stdout write failures without a cleanup owner must return exit 1 with safe text, while owner-priority regressions remain green. Then affected package tests/typechecks, the complete Node 22 McpFn release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 8 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: every target-controlled value that occupies authored report structure must be proven unchanged by the active live-session redactor before emission; a structural collision must make the report safely incomplete. Every post-execution conformance output or persistence failure is an operational failure with fixed safe text and exit 1, while an existing retryable cleanup owner remains authoritative.\n- Producers / consumers: custom targets produce arbitrary `describe().kind` values and target-owned redaction; the live `McpFnClient` owns the active redactor and structural-preservation check; the target suite reconstructs bounded JSON/JUnit artifacts consumed by libraries and `mcpfn test-target`. Official conformance produces captured streams and bounded reports consumed by `runCli`, stdout/stderr callbacks, and report persistence.\n- Failure boundaries: suite projection after scenario execution but before credential cleanup; reinsertion of the raw target discriminator after descriptor payload redaction; final JSON/JUnit serialization; conformance stdout/stderr callbacks and report-file writes; cleanup-owner priority over secondary I/O failures.\n- Coordinated repair: validate the dynamic target kind with the live client's shared `preserveArtifactStructure` enforcement before reconstructing the descriptor so a collision sets the existing projection-failure path; apply the existing cleanup-aware safe runtime-output wrapper to the complete conformance stdout/stderr/report operation with a conformance-specific fixed message.\n- Verification criteria: a custom target whose redactor treats its arbitrary kind as a credential must return a bounded incomplete report with the raw kind absent; conformance report, stdout, and stderr failures without a cleanup owner must return exit 1 with fixed safe text and omit raw I/O details, while the existing cleanup-owner priority regressions remain green. Then affected package tests/typechecks/builds, the complete Node 22 release gate, `git diff --check`, and whole-batch caller review.\n\n### Round 9 reset-trigger reassessment\n\n- Shared invariant: credential-bearing artifacts may preserve authored schema only when both structural keys and discriminator values are proven safe; if a credential collides with mandatory structure and no safe typed artifact exists, the producer must reject with credential-free runtime failure rather than emit the credential as a key. Redaction/serialization failures must retain truthful safe taxonomy, and a failing diagnostic sink must never replace an already classified runtime outcome.\n- Producers / consumers: credential providers and target secret scopes produce protected variants; the shared testing scrubber preserves report/inspector envelope keys; `McpFnClient` produces event and diagnostic target descriptors; suites and inspector/CLI serialize consumer-visible JSON/JUnit; `runCli` classifies operational failures and writes diagnostics through an injectable stderr sink.\n- Failure boundaries: fixed-key traversal and final serialized-output validation; live target descriptor reconstruction for events/diagnostics and omission fallbacks; suite projection of hostile descriptors versus target redaction collisions; impossible-to-serialize structural-key collisions; and the outer CLI diagnostic write after an output failure is already classified.\n- Coordinated repair: reject fixed-key credential collisions at the shared scrubber with a distinguishable structural-collision signal; ensure suite callers do not replace that impossible artifact with another colliding fallback; reconstruct client target descriptors only after validating the authored `kind` key and dynamic kind value, routing failures through omission accounting; separate credential projection failure from generic descriptor/serialization failure; and make runtime-error diagnostics best effort while leaving usage-error behavior distinct.\n- Verification criteria: dire